        StaticBook("Alchemical notes", "Triplewing", "Draen's Alchemical\nPotent Potion:\nRank Potion:\nDebonair Potion:\nSmooth potion:\nDiffuse potion:\nBungling potion:\nSparkling potion:\nThin potion:\nRefined Potion:\nCharming potion:\nClear potion:\nAcrid:\n*The whole book appears to be written in a refined handwriting, that resembles closely the handwriting of an author called Ein Sarard. The book is signed by the name \"Draen Aris\"*\nAlchemy Notes\nSymbol and strengths\nSaffvil-\nSerphants Stalk-\nTippens Root-\nElf's Hair Vine-", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemy Notes I", "Maxwell", "Sleeping Draught\n\n===================Wild Lettuce\nWater\n\n>Break leaves from plant\n>Steep leaves 1 hr. in hot water.\nBurn/Rash Oiuntment\n\n===================\nWild Lettuce\n\n>Break leaves from plant\n>Grind leaves into poultice\n>Add water and a whole Wild Lettuce\n\n*Helps with dry skin*\nRepitation Potion\n\n===================Water\nCaterpillar Fungus\n\n>Grind up fungus\n>Add fungus to heated water and mix well\nHealing Water I\n\n===================Water\nLily pad\n\n>Heat up water\n>Cut up Lily Pad and mix with some water\n>Pour some in wound\n\nWARNING:\n       Stings slightly\nHealing Water II\n\n===================Rose\nSpider Eye\nBone\nWater\n\n>Grind up Rose, Spider Eye, and Bone\n>Mix with water that was once snow\nHealing Water II\n                 (Cont'd)\n===================Lily Pad\nHealing Water II\n\n>Place a few drops in wound\n>Place Lily Pad on wound and bandage\n-If Lily Pad turns brown, it's healing\nPoison \n\n===================Spider Eye\nWater\n\n>Grind up spider eye add to water\nPoison II\n\n===================Posion I\n2 Spider Eyes\nBone\nRose\nDead Lily Pad\n\n>Grind up Bone, Rose, Spider Eyes, and Lily Pad\n>Mix with Poison I\nBone Pain Reliever\n\n==================\nShroom of Immortality\nWater\nLily Pad\n\n>Grind up Shroom of Immortality\n>Grind up Lily Pad\n>Mix together, then add heated water\nStomach Pain Reliever\n\n===================\nJelly Ear\nWater\nSugarcane\n\n>Grind up Jelly Ear and mix with heated water\n>Extract sugar from sugarcane and add to mixture\nDraught of Thought\n\n===================2 Magic Shrooms\nWater\nWild Lettuce\n\n>Grind Wild Lettuce into a poultice\n>Grind up Magic Shrooms\n>Mix together, add water, then heat\nLimb Pain Reliever\n\n==================\n? Shroom ?\nWater\nLily Pad\n\n>Grind up Lily Pad\n>Grind up.. ?Shroom?\n>Mix together, then add to heated water\nEnergy Potion\n\n===================\n2 Cocoa Beans\nSugarcane\nWater\n\n>Grind up Cocoa beans\n>Exctact Sugar from Sugarcane\n>Mix together, then add to heated water\nEnergy Potion II\n\n==================\n3 Cocoa Beans\n2 Sugar Cane\nWater\n\n>Steep Sugarcane in water for half an hour.\n>Grind up Cocoa Beans\n>Get rid of Sugarcane then add Cocoa Bean\nPain Reliever\n\n==================\nTippens Root", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemy Recipes", "Rosso", "Sleeping Draught\nBurn/Rash Oiuntment*\nRespiration Potion\nHealing Water I\nHealing Water II\nHealing water II\nPoison\nPoison II\nBone Pain Reliever\nStomach Pain Reliever\nDraught of Thought\nLimb Pain Reliever\nEnergy Potion\nEnergy Potion II\nPain Reliever\nAlchemy Recipes", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemy Recipes", "Jistuma", "Sleeping Draught\nBurn/Rash Oiuntment*\nRespitation Potion\nHealing Water I\nHealing Water II\nHealing water II\nPoison\nPoison II\nBone Pain Reliever\nStomach Pain Reliever\nDraught of Thought\nLimb Pain Reliever\nEnergy Potion\nEnergy Potion II\nPain Reliever\nAlchemy Recipes\n              Sven\n    The Art of Brewing\nII. Brew Testing\nAfter ((2 days)) of aging Rubber w/ wheat has ((0:45 sec of Waterbreathing))\nAfter ((3 days)) of aging Rubber w/ wheat has ((0:50 sec of Waterbreathing))\nAfter ((2 days)) Rubber w/ apple has ((0:50 sec Waterbreathing))\nAfter ((2 days)) of aging Rubber w/ pumk. has ((0:50 sec Haste))\nAfter ((2 days)) of aging Rubber w/ carrot has ((0:50 sec Waterbreathing))\nAfter ((2 days)) of aging unnatural w/ carrot has no effects.\nJournal Log #1\nwould die of blindness. Her beauty was like the light from a moon casted down on a lake, simply stunning. As I followed her I fell from a tree and awoke in darkness. I then rushed back to Salvus and stayed there. \nfather's journal which contained the secrets to a ancient artifact. The ingredients were:\n*A different handwriting appears here*\nknown what you were... I woulden't have brought you here. I'm sorry about your friend and your house. I feel so bad about all of this. I am so sorry Tom... I hope i can see you  again to talk to you in person. Just... be careful of the Valiants...\nJournal Log #2", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemy Recipes", "connor428", "Table of Contents:\n\nPg. 2: Mending Potion\nPg. 3: Strength Potion\nPg. 4: Potion of Night V.\nPg. 5: Poison\nPg. 6: Nausea Poison\nMending Potion:\n\n1x Serpent's Stalk\n1x Tippen's Root\n1x Water\n\nThis potion heals minor cuts of the user. Can be poured into small wounds or consumed. After consumption, the user felt a slight bit weak.\nStrength Potion:\n\n1x Miner's Helmet\n1x Flame Tongue\n1x Goblin's Ivy\n1x Water\n\nPotion gives the user strength for a very short amount of time. WARNING: This thing kicks like a mule!!!\nPotion of Night Vision\n\n1x Egg\n1x Swamp Blossom\n1x Water\n\nAllows user to see in almost pitch black. However, he may find that he has trouble when mining minerals as he will have a bit of fatigue.\nPoison:\n\n1x Spider Eye\n\nIt's poison. Does anything else even need to be said?\nNausea Poison:\n\n1x Rotten Flesh\n1x Goblin's Ivy\n1x Water\n\nIt's like poison. But it makes you hungry and nauseaus.", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemy Recipies", "Leyu (Domainoft)", "You've been derped.", ""),
        StaticBook("Alchemy Theory", "gojou", "\n\nTenadil’s Journal on Alchemy \n-----------------\n\n\nTheory\nReferences \n\n-Intro to Alchemy\n\n-Tales of a traveling Alchemist (Volume I)\n\n-Tales of a traveling Alchemist (Volume I-2): Elements of Alchemy \n\n-Alchemy Recipes (unknown origin) \nForward \n\nSince my mother has passed away, I have come to live in my fathers keep, mostly filled with his men. To keep myself out of trouble and away from boredom, I have begun studying the latest set of books that I acquired prior to \nleaving Malinor. This Journal records my experiences in learning the basics of alchemy, and will hopefully, as it nears completion, record my successful attempts and useful recipes.\nChapter.1: Review of literature \n\nThe Mentality \n\nAfter reviewing the aforementioned literature, I have concluded a single yet crucial truth, Alchemy is Dangerous! I do not mean that it is necessarily going\nto get yourself killed by practicing it, but, care and diligence should be maintained to the upmost when performing alchemy and using its fruits.\n\nWith this in mind, I have also concluded that patience and persistence are need, and both crucial \ntraits required to perform Alchemy. It can also be concluded that a certain level of acknowledgment that you will probably end up causing yourself harm is also required. In the Tales of a Traveling Alchemist, the “eyebrows” are thoroughly described as \nbeing forfeit in many situations, both at a novice and experienced level of skill. \n\nIt is my belief that someone who wishes to practice alchemy should have all of these traits prior to beginning.\nThe Principle \n\nAccording to The tales of a traveling alchemist, one of the most fundamental principles is that of equivalent exchange. This is (as it sounds) were to get something, you must trade something else. This means that in short, \nyou cannot gain something for nothing. In volume I-2, this is achieved through using symbolism of the four basic Alchemical elements in your ingredients:\n-> Fire\n-> Earth\n-> Water\n-> Air\n\nAs volume I-2 of Tales of a Traveling Alchemist covers this in depth, I shall avoid repetition for the time being; suffice it to say that each element represents different features that can be applied to Alchemy. \n\n\nEffects that each \nelement represents within Alchemy are achieved by using ingredients that are representative of the element. This would theoretically allow an Alchemist to design something purely on the idea of symbolism without ever making something. It also leads on to\nhow one might work out how to modify or fix his work without actually touching it.\n\nThe Principle of elements extends further to balance within an alchemical solution. If a solution uses more than one component than required, then\naccording to Tales of a traveling alchemist, this can be the source of the aforementioned danger.\n\nThis elemental unbalance can be translated into a list of ingredients effectively, and may be considered the cause of a poorly created \nand mixed solution. I believe that checking the quantities of each ingredient, and what the represent to ensure that one element does not prevail over the other is the key to successful alchemy \n\nTo summarise the principle of alchemy \nas I have understood it, you must achieve your end result through the exchange of ingredients that are a representation of the four alchemical elements.\n Chapter.3: The Elements \n\nBased on my understanding of Tales of a Traveling Alchemist, volume I-2, I will try to convey the principles behind the different elements and how they can provide different effects.\n\nFire\n------------\n\nApparently this is the most useful and frequently used elements, and can easily represent the following effects among other things.\n\nFire by nature can destroy, but this can\nalso be seen as unmaking or even cleansing! It can also be used to symbolise may emotions, such as an inflamed passion for something, anger and rage, anger, bravery and courage!\nEarth\n------------\n\nEarth can be considered a representation of strength (such as metals), as well as endurance As earth is also one of the things needed to grow plants, it can be considered a symbol of growth, life \nand healing.\n Water\n------------\n\nAs one may have guessed, water is the base of most solutions, I believe this is in partial due to the way water moves and flows, specifically the way it carries and transports things. \nThis is likely the main reason as to why water is used as a “delivery system” if you like; in most solutions. It has also been referenced as being a purifier and healer, and I belive this is also something to keep in mind with alchemy.\nAir\n------------\n\nAs I understand it, air is the most difficult element to represent, but no less important. By observing nature, one can see that wind is both slow and fast, and like water, flows, so it can represent travel, or speed\nAs air is anywhere and everywhere, it can also be used as a since of presence, or even sight of many places (described as a all-seeing).\n Chapter.4: The Design - Balance \n\nAs I mentioned earlier, alchemy can be dangerous, especially with an unbalanced solution. I believe the next part of theory that requires explanation is the preparation of a solution. Firstly I will\nwill cover my theory on how to organise the parts of a solution that elements represent, and give example of how they may be balanced.\nExample Mix 1: \nFire -> 3 Parts\nEarth -> 0 Parts\nWater -> 2 Parts\nAir -> 0 Parts\n\nIn Example 1, there is less water than fire, I believe that this would be explosively unstable\nExample Mix 2: \nFire -> 3 Parts\nEarth -> 0 Parts\nWater -> 2 + 1 Parts\nAir -> 0 Parts\n\nExample 2 is the same as Example 1, but with more water elements, thus becoming stable and theoretically useable!\nThe important thing to remember between Examples 1 and 2, is that although more representation of the water element was added, it does not necessarily have to be water itself!\nExample Mix 3: \nFire -> 3 Parts\nEarth -> 2 Parts\nWater -> 5 Parts\nAir -> 0 Parts\n\nIn example 3, there is far more water used than any other element, but say that I wanted 4 parts of each element?\n\nExample Mix 4: \nFire -> 3-1+2 Parts\nEarth -> 2-1+3 Parts\nWater -> 5 -1 Parts\nAir -> 0 Part\n\nThe solution I have provided in Example 4 is slightly more complex. TO get the correct amount of water, 1part of it was \nremoved, and with it, 1 part of every other element within the mix. This then requires additional elements to be added to get the correct mix.\n Chapter.4: Prologue \n\nI hope that I have summarised my theoretical knowledge of alchemy to a point were another can understand my conclusions, however I recommend that anyone reading this also reads the referenced books at the start. \nAs I have found myself a teacher for alchemy, I plan to begin creating a similar journal containing my practical experiences.\n\n\n\nSigned: \nTenadil McHarnish", ""),
