        StaticBook("Earth evocation", "Vuln Shadeleaf", "Seek out Joe Orman in Malinor for further instructions.\n(2/13/14) My name is Zara, I was born different. Pale skin and ice blue eyes. I was born into a cult that worshiped a human named 'Zarah\" not to be confused with me. We were named Zara by the cult leader, and said to be the next heir to her 'following' it\nwas nothing special, a pure hoax to trick young men into doing as she pleased. I was cursed with that fate. When the cult went down, I was the primary target by the ones whom apposed us. I was taken and sold into slavery, the brand on my thigh states my \nname and my former owner. I will not state this mans name to protect his privacy and my own freedom. Today I realised that, there are many out there who are the same as me, hated by those who truly do not understand. I saw a woman today like that. I \nwanted to speak to her, but I was too shy. I honestly have no idea how I plan to go about this from now on. But I do plan to speak to her in the future. I will, speak to her in the future.", ""),
        StaticBook("Eblonism", "CCCP_Zhulik", "-------------\nThe Glorious\nEblonist Manifesto\n-------------\n\nBy: Voron Rovyk\n\nDedicated to the future civilization of Adunia and the well-being of her people.\nChapter 1: Royalty\n\nIn my ongoing experiences with politics in Adunia I realised something, the leadership position is constantly filled with spineless imperialistic fools who serve no one but themselves.\n\nThe king should be --\n-- elected by a vote within the Thanes council, chosen over ability rather than heredity. Once the nominees are voted for the people vote for the best king. Once elected the king's sole job would be to rule fairly and just while seeking wisdom along --\n-- with his original task on the Thanes council. He would then be given two votes on the council rather than one.\n\nThe king would not be able to hold his own land, rather he'd be given a home so he would not be greedy.\nChapter 2:\nThe Thanes Council\n\nThe Thanes Council would be members that best suited and served the task given to them by the people. They would not have jurisdiction publicly to arrest or give orders. \nAll civil matters --\n-- must be voted on within the council. Therefore no council member could declare marshal law or order the death of other Thanes. All thanes would have equal power.\n\n\nChapter 3:\nThe Military.\n\nThe Grand Marshal title would be abolished from the thanes council, the military's leaders would consist of the thanes voted on by the council for a single specific conflict, thus when the conflict was over --\n-- the leadership and commanding of the military would be null for the individual.\n\nThe guard force would be lead by two individuals to balance power, one villainous and one noble. They'd be forced to abide by their code which stated they could --\n-- not execute without trial. Torture however would be legal before a trial only if evidence pointed towards the individual and the guards were given permission by the Thanes.\n\nIn times of war all citizens would be required to fight --\n-- for the glory of the 'Motherland'. They'd be put in the special partisan regiment. Women would tend to the wounded and defend the keep, men would go out and fight until death unless instructed to retreat for tactical\npurposes. All deserters --\n-- would be shot.\n\nHowever Adunia would also have a private military that would offer service to any nation or individual that pays aslong as it does not effect Adunia negatively. The PMCs would use contract, service being abolished once --\n-- each contract is complete. Private Militaries would be funded and operated by it's founder or founders. \n\nMilitary conflict should be the last concern of the council of thanes.\nChapter 4:\nEconomy\n\nThe task of running the economy would be given to the Treasurer position in the thanes council. Adunia would focus on farm products and small time industry aswell as small free-lance --\n-- businesses. However taxes would be collected by the government depending on overall wealth so that a poor man would not pay as much as a rich man and vice versa.\n\nThe farm would sell food to foreign entities along with --\n-- supplying the citizens with a steady flow of food. The farms would be government regulated. Carrots would be the number one produced comodity.\n\nIndustry would be light, mainly smelting and boat-making.\nChapter 5:\nProperty and Nobility:\n\nProperty would be based on loyalty rather than nobility or heredity. The more the citizen contributes to the country the more property they recieve. Example: A person moves to Adunia and they recieve --\n-- a slum-house for one elven week. In that time they must prove their loyalty to the nation and activity within civil disputes. After this week if proven to be loyal they would be given a middle-class home after enlisting in either the Guards or \n--\n--domestic services.\n\nAfter ten elven weeks of constant contribution an individual would be given a plot of land to do as they wish, along with owning their previous home.\nChapter 6:\nClans\n\nClans would become a tradition custom however they would not have a say in the government, the Thanes council would. A clan would simply be an extended family of individuals that live in a common household.\nA clan's political power would be limited to the influence that they had on a council member that belonged to their clan.\n\nNote: Clan manors would be for a single, direct family of the clan chieftan rather than the entire clan as a whole.\nChapter 7:\nCivil Rights\n\nAll citizens would be treated equal and would be given the right of free speech, however contradictions may be given in return by the government. All citizens that do not belong in the military --\n-- or Thanes council would be given the same political status, slaves would be considered citizens thus given the right to vote, along with those who have a different race, or religion. Citizenship would be given after the one-week loyalty process, --\n-- however citizenship may be removed for inactivity and lack of service to the nation.\n\nDiscrimination would still be legal, however it would not mean the individual wouldn't have equal rights.\nChapter 8:\nCrown Authority Laws:\n\nThere would be no Crown Authority Laws which would give the king or ruler more power. The king would simply be a figure-head and have another task ontop of his original council task. \nChapter 9:\nAdunia Itself.\n\nAdunia should be renamed to something the people decide on in a massive vote. The name Adunia is commonly looked down upon by foreigners and with a new government comes a new country. The --\n-- Thane council positions are suggested below:\n\nDiplomacy\nLabor\nForeign Conflict\nCivil Conflict\nSteward\nReligion\nTreasurer\nChapter 10:\nThe End\n\nWith this guide-line Adunia, or any other nation may become productive and safe. \n\nHail the Motherland and the people for which the government Serves.", ""),
        StaticBook("EC: Vol. I", "NiceGuyNorman", "Encyclopedia Cryptologica:\n\nA Look Into the World of Supernatural Entities\n\nVOLUME I\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWritten by Samuel Geminine\nTABLE OF CONTENTS:\n- Ghoul\n- Alghoul\n- Drowner\n- Gravelkind\n- Frog–Man\n- Troll\n- Boar–Man\n- Dragon\n- The Northern Menace\n- Voidwalker / Dark One\n- Harpy\n- Cockatrice\nGHOUL:\n\nA reanimated corpse, now an undead creature. The ghoul is the most common of the necrophage family, and their weakness is silver, and fire. They appear to have a lust for human flesh. The standard undead of the necrophage class.\n\nDanger Level: LOW\nALGHOUL:\n\nA significantly stronger type of ghoul, standing larger than the regular necrophage and packing a much larger punch. I hypothesize that the alghoul is either an evolved ghoul, or the reanimated form of a larger man.\n\n\nDanger Level: MEDIUM\nDROWNER:\n\nThe corpses of drowned individuals that have been reanimated with a parasite to become necrophage ghouls. They lunge onto their unsuspecting victims from riverbanks, dragging them to the deeps to their untimely demise.\n\nDanger Level: MEDIUM\nGRAVELKIND:\n\nThe most dangerous type of necrophage. A necromancer's failed experiment, the gravelkind, or cemetaur, can create other ghouls with corpses. They are highly intelligent, and not to be underestimated.\n\n\nDanger Level: HIGH\nFROGMAN:\n\nA bipedal, man–sized creature, with the appearance of a humanoid frog. It has terrorized the lands of Lenfarthing of recent. They appear to be intelligent, and their weakness is dry skin. They are found in pools of water.\n\nDanger Level: LOW\nTROLL:\n\nA large, fat creature, with low-light sight and the ability to speak in common tongue. They make their home in the Witch Woods, in a large cave. Their weakness is light of any form. They humanoid in figure.\n\n\nDanger Level: MEDIUM\nBOAR–MAN:\n\nOtherwise known as the Bohra, these creatures make their home in the harsh north. They take the appearance of a man with a boar's head, and follow a pagan god. They hunt in packs, and can be killed with regular weaponry.\n\n\nDanger Level: MEDIUM\nDRAGON:\n\nA large, winged reptile, capable of breathing fire and taking to the air with flying expertise. Dragons, or drakes, are plagued with greed, and can live for hundreds of years, terrorizing countryside villages.\n\n\nDanger Level: HIGH\nTHE NORTHERN MENACE:\n\nThese creatures make their home in the harsh, mysterious North lands. They come in a variety of disfigured, horrifying appearances, and are known to raid and kidnap. They possess high intelligence and strength.\n\n\nDanger Level: HIGH\nVOIDWALKER / DARK ONE:\n\nA tall, black-skinned creature, with elongated limbs. They become aggresive when one looks into their glowing eyes, and are capable of 'blinking', or shifting from one place to the other in an instant.\n\n\nDanger Level: MEDIUM\nHARPY:\n\nA terrible creature, the harpy takes the appearance of a haggard woman, with the wings of a bat. The harpies are known to plague towns with their screeching and ungodly acts, and are a nuisance, to say the least.\n\n\nDanger Level: LOW\nCOCKATRICE:\n\nA feathery, avian creature, taking the appearance of an impossibly large chicken. Do not let it's comical appearance deceive you; the cockatrice's mere gaze can turn a man to stone ...\n\n\n\nDanger Level: MEDIUM", ""),
