        StaticBook("FirstCity:Everal", "Domainoft", "The History of Everal, the First City of the Elves\n-Retold by Leyu'Maehr\nVaehaven\n*Strange Numbers\n((3/3/13))\nA long time ago the First Race crossed one of the Great Oceans of Aegis to arrive on a beach. This beach was bestowed with a Magikal power from a long gone time. It let all plant grow abundantely there and in the surrounding\nareas. The sand there made glass as hard as steel and that which would never break. So when the First Race, later known as Elves, planted their magikal seeds... great towering trees grew in their place. Ordinary Fyre could not scorch these\ntrees, for the magik embeded in them was still new. The Elves thrived in the new land along with the trees, spreading its seeds wherever they went, creating an Empire that Dwarfed all others previousely known. The Elves lived inside the trunks of\nthese towering trees, singing to them to help them grow. And all was peaceful. Then one day many centuries later, a Astronomical Strom swept across the land, Felling the Magikal Trees where they stood. Lightning stroke the exposed Earth, melting the\ngound and forming pools of lava. Great Fyre, fueled by the dark energys of the storm, burnt all of the remaining trees. Only a few kilometers remained. This is where the Elves sheltered during the storm. They named this area Evaral, meaning\nProtected in their lost language. They tried to spread the seeds of the Magikal trees once more, but the Earth still remembered the horrors of the storm and wouldn't let them grow. And when this area became overpopulated, the Elves started to\nreach for the sky! They trees grew until they touched the highest planes of the Atmosphere! And all was at peace one again. The Elves built walls of enchanted wood around Evaral, to protect the city from new predators and spirits that rose with the\nStorm. And then a terrible thing happened. Some of the Elves dissented from this way of life, as wonderful as it was. They became greedy and wanted more land, ores, and resources. One by one the Dissenters left Evaral, preferring the\nnew forests beyond its border. They spread out far accross the land, struggling to cope with their new homes. Eventually even they settled down and formed small villages, under no ruler. Some even evolved into other races, forgetting\nwhat they left behind. By this time only a few families remained in Evaral, and they did not know what to do. They missed the kinship of the other Elves, so they decided to leave too. But before they left each family took with them a seed from the\nfirst tree, which happened to reside in Evaral. This tree was very old and wrought with homes. And its spirit was sad too, for it missed the Elves that left. It shed its bark to help the remaining Elves in their journeys, forming impenetrable armor to\nhelp the remaining elves in their journeys. This armor now resides in the Tomb of Evaral, as the First Tree's spirit reclaimed it. And then none were left in Evaral. However over the centuries the seed still could grow, but the land never accepted them.\nUntil one day, many centuries later, it came in the hands of a young druid. The great city of Evaral's location is hidden from the world, but many believe it is still ou t there. Perhaps one day, a great hero will stumble upon it. And bring it to power\nonce again.", ""),
        StaticBook("Fis", "Cir'dian", "\nFishipedia\n\nBy Sen Nymphea\n\nBonefish -\n\nPaper thin skin with countless bones visible. Choking hazard if consumed. \n\nAvg. weight: 1lb 6oz\n\nEffects: Likely to make you choke on a bone\n\nTreefish -\n\nLong and fairly thin. Scales have wooden bark like texture.\n\n\nAvg. weight: 3lbs 4oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nLakefish -\n\nSlick, and oily. Very strong tail and tends to flail if landed.\n\n\nAvg. weight: 2lbs 2oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nMagikarp -\n\nDull orange scales and large eyes. Does very little, even if landed.\n\nAvg. weight: 2lbs 2oz\n\nEffects: None, though, may splash you in the face\n\nStonefish -\n\nTough, stone like skin and very heavy. Difficult and unpleasant to eat.\n\nAvg. weight: 33lbs 8oz\n\nEffects: Skin starts to feel heavy and solid\n\nMackeral -\n\nLong and thin body with fairly large mouth. Black, stripy pattern along its back.\n\nAvg. weight: 1lb 7oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nRagefish -\n\nGrey with reddish tint. Always seems to be angry.\n\n\nAvg. weight: 3lbs 4oz\n\nEffects: Sudden urge to break things\n\nBass -\n\nSilvery/grey tone with 2 fins along its back.\nHuge mouth but small teeth.\n\nAvg. weight: 4lbs 0oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nSunfish -\n\nGlossy, vibrant yellow scales. Only seems to swim about during daylight.\n\nAvg. weight: 4lbs 7oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nMooneye -\n\nBluish/grey scales that glow under moonlight. Only seen at night.\n\nAvg. weight: 2lbs 6oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nDogfish -\n\nVery fluffy with patterns that vary depending on location.\n\n\nAvg. weight: 2lbs 1oz\n\nEffects: Odd temptation to bark like a dog\n\nGoldfish -\n\nFairly small, but heavy fish made of seemingly living gold.\n\n\nAvg. weight: 17lbs 2oz\n\nEffects: Tends to leave gold lumps in mouth\n\nSwordfish -\n\nRather large and strong. Dangerously sharp blade at the tip of its mouth.\n\nAvg. weight: 9lbs 6oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nRibbontail -\n\nSubtle pink colour with red fins. Tail forks and trails like ribbons.\n\n\nAvg. weight: 2lbs 3oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nCod -\n\nVarious colours and sizes, usually yellow and green. Often found near The Sea.\n\nAvg. weight: 8lbs 5oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nSardine -\n\nTiny, silver scaled bodies. Usually swim about in massive schools.\n\nAvg. weight: 0lbs 2oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nScomber -\n\nSimilar to Mackeral.\nVaries slightly in colour and size.\n\n\nAvg. weight: 2lbs 2oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nNemofish -\n\nVibrant orange with white stripes. Always seems to swim toward The Ocean.\n\nAvg. weight: 1lb 1oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nWormfish -\n\nVery, very long and thin. Doesn't actually seem to have a head.\n\n\nAvg. weight: 1lb 9oz\n\nEffects: Nausea and paranoia\n\nSpittail -\n\nVicious and scraggy appearance. Tail end looks and smells rotten.\n\nAvg. weight: 1lb 3oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nAnglefish -\n\nVery angular, almost sqaure body. Even the eyes are diamond shaped.\n\nAvg. weight: 3lbs 2oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nMudsucker -\n\nFlat and round fish, quite like a dish. Smells quite similar to cow manure.\n\nAvg. weight: 4lbs 0oz\n\nEffects: Feel nauseus, infatuated and starved\n\nPerch -\n\nOften found very close to The Sea. Fights very hard if hooked or caught.\n\nAvg. weight: 5lbs 3oz\n\nEffects: Very filling and greatly rejuvinates\n\nHerring -\n\nRather small fish found in large schools in The Sea.\n\n\nAvg. weight: 0lbs 9oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nBarrelfish -\n\nBurly and round fish with an oddly brown tone to its scales.\n\n\nAvg. weight: 5lbs 12oz\n\nEffects: None, though, fun to roll around with\n\nFlounder -\n\nFlat, sand coloured body. Tends to swim along riverbeds.\n\n\nAvg. weight: 2lbs 8oz\n\nEffects: Somehow gives ability to breath under water.\n\nCatfish -\n\nSlender and agile swimmer. Has long whiskers along its face.\n\nAvg. weight: 3lbs 15oz\n\nEffects: Urge to meow like a cat\n\nThe One Fish -\n\nVery large and majestic. Fish of all kinds tend to follow it.\n\n\nAvg. weight: 28lbs 5oz\n\nEffects: -Unknown-\n\nMonkfish -\n\nVery fat and greedy looking fish with sharp teeth. Could easily bite a finger off.\n\nAvg. weight: 9lbs 12oz\n\nEffects: Cuts and bruises will quickly heal themselves\n\nOinkfish -\n\nScaleless, pink body. Face looks almost identical to that of a pig.\n\nAvg. weight: 4lbs 1oz\n\nEffects: Urge to squeel or oink like a pig\n\nEel -\n\nSnake like and sinister black. Glides through water and slithers on land.\n\nAvg. weight: 3lbs 15oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nTuna -\n\nVery large and rarely found far from sea. Very strong and difficult to land.\n\nAvg. weight: 23lbs 3oz\n\nEffects:\n\nRainbowfish -\n\nBeautiful scales that shine every colour. Very rarely even seen.\n\nAvg. weight: 5lbs 2oz\n\nEffects: Said to boost physical capabilities\n\nStingfish -\n\nHostile and dangerous. The thorn on its tale smells of rotting meat.\n\n\nAvg. weight: 7lbs 10oz\n\nEffects: Severe sickness\n\nMooshroomfish -\n\nLooks similar to the rare Mooshroom cow. Even has mushrooms growing on its tail.\n\nAvg. weight: 2lbs 6oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nCarp -\n\nFairly large with 2 long whiskers. Tends to drift along riverbanks.\n\nAvg. weight: 7lbs 0oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nArrowtooth -\n\nFairly large with red/grey scales. Teeth are short and \nvery pointy.\n\nAvg. weight: 6lbs 11oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nBluegill -\n\nScales range in shades of blue and has a wide blue mouth.\n\n\nAvg. weight: 1lb 4oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nTonguefish -\n\nHas an unusually long and strong tongue with which it catches smaller fish with.\n\nAvg. weight: 4lbs 8oz\n\nEffects: None\n\nSilverfish -\n\nCan't actually tell if this is a fish or a bug, but it's now sqaushed to the page...\n\nAvg weight: >0lbs 1oz\n\nEffects: None\n\n        ->]]]]|==\n\nBlackfish -\n\nSkin is so dark it doesn't even reflect light.\n\n\nAvg. weight: 3lbs 2oz\n\nEffects:", ""),
        StaticBook("Fish and you", "simonlk", "Fish and you!\n\nThis guide will tell you about all the fish around Anthos, and all their effects! Some fish are only found in specific biomes, and during special times of the day, so beware of that! This guide is brought to you by Olak Stevenson, and Jimmy\nQhyain. Time to get started! To start fishing you will need either a fishing net, or a fishing rod. A fishing net is crafted like this:\nOXX\nOXX\n/OO\nA x equals string, whilst a slash equals a stick.\nA fishing rod is crafted like this:\nOO/\nO/X\n/OX\nx equals string, and slash equals sticks.\n\nNow that you got yourself a fishing rod, or a fishing net, it is finally time to start fishing! There are over 80\ndifferent fish you can catch! If you are using a fishing net, simply throw it in the water, and wait for all the fish foolishly swimming into your net. If you use a net, you will also over time catch different kinds of junk and valueables. If you use a\nfishing rod, you will be able to fish for a longer amount of time, but you will catch less fish, and wont be able to find junk. That pretty much sums up the basics, the rest of this book will list all the different fish, and junk you can get fishing.\n                 Fish:\nCarp - A raw fish\nHerring - A raw fish\nTuna - A large raw fish\nCod - A raw fish\nSardine - A raw fish\nAnglefish - A raw fish\nRibbontail - A raw fish\nBass - A large raw fish\nLongfin - A raw fish\n           Special fish:\nMagikarp - It appears to be the most boring fish in history - But is delicious cooked! Has no special effects.\nSunfish - It sparkles under the sunlight - when cooked the scales shine faintly in darkness. Has no effects.\nCowfish - A raw, white fish with brown spots. It seems to moan like a cow when you eat it, even when it is dead and cooked.\nWormfish - The fish looks more like a worm, thin and flat. Doesnt look very edible. When aten you feel squirmy, and nervous.\nMooneye - It sparkles under the moonlight. When cooked the scales reflect your image like mirrors. Nothing special seems to happen when aten.\nMudsucker - The fish looks round, and smells disgusting. Doesnt look very edible. When I ate it I seemed to puke,\nand my stomach rumbled. Definately not a good fish to eat.\n      Junk/treasure:\n\nMelon seeds\nWaterlogged journal\nFlint\nCarrot\nSpider eye\nSlimeball\nTowel\nPaper\nSugarcane\nBone\nGold nugger\nString\nInk sack\nLeather\nSoggy boots\nStick\nPotato\nOak sapling\nCobweb\nClay", ""),
        StaticBook("Fishes", "Marcitos123", "-----------------------Kinds of Fish---\n-------------------Greetings reader, my name is Bennidict Chapman, this is my book and only mine.\nHerring = Normal\nMagikarp = Rare\nLakefish = Sweet\nMooneye = Bitter\nWormfish = Disgusting\n((Hunger/Nausea))\nTreefish = Normal\nCutthroat = Sharp\nRibbontail = Normal\nBonefish = Cough\nGoldfish = Spit-gold\nDogfish = Weird\nAnglefish = Normal\nFlounder = Amazing!\n((Water Breathing))\nCowfish = Steak Taste\nEel = Large and Soft\nPike = Normal\nMudsucker = Horrible\n(Hunger,Slowness II)\nMackerel = Good\nOinkfish = Pork Taste\nSunfish = Normal\nNemofish = Rainbow\nPerch = Great\n((Strength))\nScomber = Normal\nTheOneFish = Unique\n((Invisibility))", ""),
        StaticBook("Fishys O Maliner", "MushyNinja", "The  purpose O 'his book be to recor' any an all diferent types of Fishys an Fishlings. Me will describe ow they look raw an cooked. All O' these fishys come from Maliner Rivers.\nSquid Tentacles\nMooneye\nCod\nBonefish\nSunfish\nTreefish\nFlounder\nSalmon\nPike\nRibbonTail\nPiranha", ""),
        StaticBook("Flower Princess", "Soresan", "There once was a beautiful Human princess in a far away land, who would walk her kingdom and small all the flowers in the land. The flowers were every color and every shape imaginable and stretched for miles upon miles.\nThough this princess seemed kind and fair, she would seduce men by the dozens, and sacrafice them in the name of Iblees. One day, an old crow walked into the court of the Princess.\n\"My son! My son!\" She yelled. \"He is dead because of you! I raised him from a babe to a man and that is all nothing because of you!\" The king of the land, taken aback and insulted, ordered the woman put in the stocks. \nThe day before the crow's execution for treason, the Princess decided to pay her one final insult. She walked as she always did, smelling her flowers and smiling. She finally got to the woman, and grinned from ear to ear.\n\"Ha! I am Princess, and you are but an old woman. My subjects will love me forever, but they will forget you instantly. \" The crow growled at her, and snapped. \"They will find you even more lovely and remember you forever. I curse \nyour name, Princess, to be destroyed by that you most love, like my dear son was!\" The Princess thought nothing of it, and walked off. Days after the crow was executed, the sun shone far brighter than it had in days past.\nThis made the Princess walk further than normal and sniff her flowers twice. She walked the entire kingdom twice, sniffing every flower. Satisfied, she began walking. When she got to the stocks, she stopped.\nShe tried to lift her foot up over and over, but it would not move! She looked down, and gasped. There were roots growing from her feet, and planting themselves down into the ground. She franticly tried to free herself, but\nthe more she struggled, the more the vines moved up her body. Soon, her entire body was covered in green. She couldn't moon, and soon her head turned yellow, her eyes shut forever, and her hair turned to petals. \nThe crow ad cursed her to be that what she loved most. Her grave was her haven, and she would be remembered forever; As the biggest flower ever known.", ""),
        StaticBook("For Abner", "laurannex", "Abner,\n\n\n     I want you to know that when I said I could take care of Ariela on my own, I didn't mean that I wanted you to stay out of our lives. Thats probably the last thing I need happening. You are the closest\nthing I have to me right now, besides Ariela herself. I was just frustrated and upset and... yeah. I still don't really know how to deal with all of this... I think I am able to handle Oslo being gone a bit better now, but it still hurts. I guess I just\ntook all of my anger and frustration and, uh, upset-ness(?) on you. \n     I guess that what I'm trying to say is that I'm sorry. I'm  sorry that you have to deal with all of this... You shouldn't have to be the one helping me take care of Ariela. \nI'm sorry for anything I've said or done that may have hurt you... I'm really, really, really, really, really, really, really, really, really, really, really, really, really, really, really, really, REALLY             SORRY!\n\n*Three words here have been crossed out. At closer inspection, the first letter of the second word is an 'L', and the last letter of the last word is a 'u'.*\n\nSincerely,\n\n             Evangeline", ""),
        StaticBook("for alley cat", "Nugosaurus", "nug was here nug was here nug was here gib mi shiniez if you don't i find you and kill you\n-love nug", ""),
        StaticBook("For Charlotte", "Your Love", "To my dearest Charlotte,\n\nIf you are reading this, it means I've passed on. I am with the Creator now, among his faithful in the Seven Skies. Do not cry for me. I am in a better place, where hope is always new. I will see you here \nsome day, when you will take your side in the White Keep with me. My love for you will never die, and may yours never die for me. Please, always remember me. But do not fail to search for love. I want you to be happy, that is my ultimate wish.\nEnclosed is the Briarwood family treasure. A bracelet. My mother's, then Cosette's, and now yours. \n\nFarewell,\n\nMarcus\n\n\n\n", ""),
        StaticBook("For Dun Irongut", "Raomir", "Dun are ye safe? Did ye travel out? The battle was hard, but I found some thing fer ye....\n-Raomir", ""),
        StaticBook("For Felix", "gorgo_empire", "~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~         A Collection\n             of\n      Songs, Poems,\n            and\n        Limericks\n-------------------By: Posco T. Bramble\n*The author's signature appears to have been written in blue ink*\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nPreface\n-------------------Before the list may begin, the definition of a Song, Poem, and Limerick must be stated -just for those whom know not of merry word play and the like- so without further fuss, I shall state the definitions. \n------------------\nSong\n-------------------A metrical composition, in this case, intended for singing. Often times the words sung having a steady beat and rhyme.\n\nPoem\n-------------------A written piece that is part speech and song\nand that is usually rhythmical and often times metaphorical.\n\nLimerick\n-------------------An often times humorous, sometimes bawdy, verse of three long and two short lines rhyming aabba.\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nTo begin the list we have a merry poem detailing the joyous goings ons and beauty of Lenfarthing. The rhyme is written by Felix Featherfoot and commissioned by yours truly, Posco Bramble.\nWithout further introduction, the poem.\n-------------------\nI've heard the tale of wheat and ale\nI'm heading off to the Dales\n\nTo Lenfarthing\nOh what joy does it bring!\n \nFlaxen fields, and flaxen hair\nOh do all the ladies look so fair\nIn Lenfarthing\nWhere merry haflings sing!\n \nFriends carousing around the hearth\nLaughing, and drinking with such mirth\n \nIn Lenfarthing\nWhere full tankards cling!\nSimples days spent in the sun\nin the fields where little folk run\n \nTo Lenfarthing!\nWhere true happiness is a thing!\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nTo follow within the theme of Lenfarthing related poems -or in this case a song- we have a short rhyme detailing the happiness Lenfarthing brings.\n-------------------\nWe live in the Lenfarthing where there's ale to go around,\n\nWe live in the Lenfarthing where the peaceful ri-i-ivers sound,\n\nAs you walk through the valley, they trickle on their way,\n\nand you can't but help feel yourself having a better day, HEY!\n------------------\nThe writer of the song is currently unknown,\nhowever the rhyme is found in \"The Three Farthing's Songbook\"\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nAlthough this original copy of Song and Poems shall remain unfinished forever and it's more than likely that no one will ever read it again. I dedicate this classic print to my dearest departed friend, Felix Featherfoot. My final note to him.\n\n- Posco Bramble", ""),
        StaticBook("For Lincia", "Thynis", "I found something special I wanted to give to you dear so here it is, I hope you enjoy it.\n\n-Thynis", ""),
        StaticBook("For my new dawn", "samsan99", "To my dear friend of the new dawn.\n\nI have a request, a job that may intrest you but i wish to meet in person rather than talk over letters, Please give me your reply as soon as you can i look foward to finally metting\nmy beloved new dawn fromt the shady sorrows - mountains.", ""),
        StaticBook("Foreign envoys", "Iorwen", "To Marquis Thomas Chivay of the Westerlands, Grand Marshal of the Holy Oren Empire\n\nOur two nations have long remained somewhat isolated from one another and now even more so on account of this most perilous migration to \nwhat we here in Couentre dub 'the New World' - of course I refer to Anthos.\n\nI am told that Anthos' size is only a fraction of that of Asulon's and that it is somewhat barren in terms of natural resources. These facts alone make me question the\nneed for your good Empire to move there, though the questioning of divine will, I find, is best left to philosophers and its subjugation best left to priests.\n\nCouentre as an ostensibly neutral trading port would like to reopen diplomatic \nnegotiations and foster the prospect of an alliance with House Chivay and the dire political entity known as the Westerlands. I write to you today to ask your consent for us to send a senior ambassador, Vezian\nMarilluys to your court on behalf of \nthe Prince of Couentre himself.\n\nWe would greatly value your consent and have no doubt that continued negotiations between Couentre and our fellows of Gaekrin, House Chivay, would be prosperous for all involved.\n\nSincerely,\n\nPatrician Vsevelod of Saxe-Coburg-Gotha, Lord High Admiral of Couentre, Vanquisher of Alnorus, High Almoner of Couentre and Pacifier of the Morcian Rye Uprising", ""),
        StaticBook("Foreing relegion", "Dork117", "This will be about a foreign belief. I am not going to try and pursuede you to follow it. I do however want you to think about it. \nThok'Dath, The Furious\ngiven to him by his former master. The shackles of his captivity still attached to his limbs, only broken. \nand the freedom of his worshippers. If he gets this, he will be glad. If he however does not get this, he will be furious and even his most trusted servants have to take care as to not step into the path of this god, for in his blind determination\nto obtain his goal, Lok’Thar will break every bone, rip off every bit of flesh and take every flicker of hope for survival for anyone who stands in his way. Offerings to this god, might be the broken shackles you once wore, or anything else resembling\nthe freedom that you have obtained. An interesting custom is that of slavers, who donate the shackles of the slaves that were freed or escaped to Lok’Thar, in hopes of keeping his fury from them for now.\nIt is pretty much clear that this belief adn patron in particular are completly about freedom.", ""),
        StaticBook("Forest Barley", "Menarra", "Forest", ""),
        StaticBook("Forgive Me", "[Bruised][Bleeding] Erin", "Khel,\n\nIt is my deepest regrets to inform you that your people have degenerated into slovering beasts. I came for a hopefully quiet visit to attempt to see if I could help. Absolute nether broke loose.\nYour daughter once again attacked me, this time in full physical force. She tackled me off a rise I was standing on near the beach, slamming me to the ground hard enough to see stars. It took all my strength not to stab her with the blade I keep hidden.\nI screamed for her to leave me, that I never did anything to harm me. However she beat my face and neck without mercy until she was pulled off by passerby. Screaming profanity as she was dragged off.\nI was very right in assuming our friendship was an impossible thing... If I'm going to be brutalized and have a smear campaign run against me each time I enter your presence.. I don't think friendship is something possible.\nI'm sorry, but if these conditions continue, I may have to hurt someone to defend myself, and I have already suffered too many broken bones and bloody noses to allow myself to be mauled ever again. I will not take these beatings any longer.\nEven if it is your own child, Prince. I will defend myself against any more of this brutality.\n\nI'm sorry, and hope you can someday forgive me for the storm I know is brewing within.\n\n-Erin", ""),
        StaticBook("Forgiven. moo", "Adorellan", "\"Lyweneth Visill has been forgiven for her crimes against the Elf known as Adorellan Kamura - she has done the task which was given to her and so, I believe she is to be forgiven for her crimes against myself.\nTo whoever this concerns,\nSend me a bird if you wish to speak upon the topic at hand.\nAs said - she has been forgiven and no longer shall I treat her as an enemy, she'll be treated with the same respect I give to friends and family.\"\nThe note is signed neatly,\nobviously the work of a noble, perhaps an ex-noble.*\n\n~ Signed, Adorellan Kamura.", ""),
        StaticBook("Forgot, the...", "Monk Scribe", "Forgot, the North\n\nThe road sheered through the alpine north,\nthe snowy drift sobered by the sky's gray smoldering.\nThe path grit its cobbled stone through a lonesome coat\nof dust, collected through lack of \ntravel.\n\nA crow coughed at my passing\nthe first home to an old town.\nI did not forget,\nsaid the shattered oak door, clapping, creaking,\nhinged in walls of cinder.\n\nThe town wheezed with an ashen breeze,\nblackened posts echoing\nfrom bygone structures\nlike ribs clasping shriveled lungs, and I\nscraped up the sticks that were\nthe manse atop the hill, wanting\nfor its warmth.\nAt the town's far side is the lake's empty mirror.\n\nBeyond the bridge, a great tower looms,\nit's battlements broken arund the gate's fevered maw,\na burgled sentinel fallen on winter,\nwhere I stop, before the North's \nmuted expanse.\n\n- Written in Aegis after the Fall of the North to the Undead.", ""),
        StaticBook("Friend", "Garukumus", "There is nothing\nmore wonderful,\nmore important,\nmore pleasant,\nmore joy-giving,\n\nThan\n\nSomeone who treats your most mundane smalltalk like it's a special part of their day.\n*nobody", ""),
        StaticBook("Front Door", "ParagonWarrior", "", ""),
        StaticBook("Frozen Orders", "Jallentime", "  You are being called upon to serve.\nDisguise yourself how you see fit and track down Prince David Winter of Herendul. Make him suffer, and carve the name \"Setherien\" into his flesh, but leave him alive.\nHe is seen traveling between Herendul and New Abresi frequently. \nThis is not a difficult task, but it will remind our foolish enemies of who they are dealing with. Do not fail us.\n\n                           -Mimir.", ""),
        StaticBook("Fuel Agreement", "Toriel", "Trade Agreement\n\n16 lumps of Coal per\nElven Day\n\nWhen this amount is delivered, payment of 25 minas must be made.\n\n\n     -Toriel", ""),
        StaticBook("Full Suit (job)", "Felix d'Ivri", "I would like a full suit please.\n\n\n-Felix d'Ivri\n", ""),
        StaticBook("Fun Poems", "Tantaparo", "If you are a dreamer, come in,\nIf you are a dreamer,\nA wisher, a liar,\nA hope-er, a pray-er,\nA magic bean buyer,\ncome in,\nIf you are a looker, A lover, A helper, A dancer, A worker, A kisser,\ncome in,\nIf you hate, leave.\nMillie McDeevit screamed a scream\nSo loud it made her eyebrows steam.\nShe screamed so loud\nHer jawbone broke,\nHer tongue caught fire,\nHer nostrils smoked...\nA spider lives inside my head\nWho weaves a strange and wondrous web\nOf silken threads and silver strings\nTo catch all sorts of flying things, Like crumbs of thought and bits of smiles\nAnd specks of dried-up tears,\n------Continue on--\nAnd dust of dreams that catch and cling\nFor years and years and years . . .", ""),
        StaticBook("Fun. Alchemy", "Jistuma", "The Tales of a Travelling Alchemist\n------------\nThe Fundamentals of Alchemy\n\nVolume I of V\n\nForeword:\nI have heard many a man ask why one, such as myself, would take such interest and dedication in the art of alchemy when there are ready-made spells that could do just the same, if not better, a job than an apothecary’s potion. My response to\nthis statement is rather simple, whereas one must have a certain gift for spell-slinging, rune-craft, and spirit-speaking, one mustn’t need these frivolous commodities to use a balm to mend a wound, shroud themselves in a nigh-invisible cloak\nwith the swig from a bitter bottle, or set someone ablaze with bottled flame. Alchemy is not merely reproducing a wizard’s spell, but it is shaping the spell in a way for the common man’s use. While magic still has its place in our world, Alchemy allows\nthose without the gift to reap the same benefits as any mage might.\nChapter 1: The Fundamentals of Alchemy\n------------\nThe Principle and Law of Equivalent Exchange:\n\nAs I was providing my services to a rather quaint hamlet, which also provided me information of the local flora and more of the same legends I always here of, I was approached by a man of common repute, and he produced a sarcastic, yet thought provoking, \nquestion. This question was, and I will attempt to reproduce the dialect as best as my phonetic ability will allow, “ ‘ow come, Mister, ‘hat ye’ can make all ‘dis ‘onderful stuff outta nothin’?” Now as shameful as his speech may be, the question remains\nthe same. How is it, that alchemists are capable or reshaping the spells of sorcerers for the common folk out of, seemingly, nothing? The answer to this question is simple to state, but fairly difficult to grasp, and this is the Principle of Equivalent \nExchange. Much like the money one gives to the merchant for the apple or slab of meat, one cannot have something for nothing. As the saying goes, “there is no such thing as a free brunch,” there is no such thing as a free potion. This fundamental rule\napplies to old alchemy, through the use of symbols and rituals, far more than what I call “Modern” alchemy. Although I will not get into ancient alchemy, I will state why this rule applies to Modern Alchemy. To craft a potion, for example a bottle of\nalchemist’s fire, one requires the materials. Much like a spell casted by a wizard, one cannot make something out of nothing. Know that to make a potion, and the possibilities are truly endless, one must take plants, objects, and other representations of \nwhat they wish to make. For example, alchemist’s fire could be best produced with an - -, a mixture of -  -, -  -, and a dash of -  -. Why would one need these items?\nThe - - provides the basic mixture of what we will make, and of course the better quality of the - - the better quality of the Alchemist’s Fire, but of course we will get further into this in a later chapter. The -  -, a -  - \nplant that grows most often in deserts, is often -  - and often associated with -  -. This provides an superb symbol for the element of -  -. The -  -, another powerful symbol of -  -, mixes well with the crushed -  -\nto produce something truly strong. The -  -, an optional portion, is another symbol of -  -, albeit both weaker than blaze -  - for both a -  - fire burns far greater than -  -.\nNow then, although you have these symbols of flame, rage, and warfare, one mustn’t simply toss objects into the metaphorical cauldron. Precision is necessary, as always. One must measure their mixture carefully, or they will give too much. And giving too\nmuch will tip the scales of Equivalent Exchange, causing unpredictable results. Why is this you might ask? When balance is achieved through Equivalent Exchanged, a mixture of the elements for the bottled spell of your desire, than all is well. But when\nyou disrupt this balance and push on the world, taking and forcing in too much of one or more element then the world will push back. Of course a word of caution is required, for one may think taking less is the best option when balance is not a certainty.\nThis is a common misconception for not providing enough of one element will result in the same conclusion, imbalance and chaos. This can be easily associated with cooking, too much of one ingredient will spoil the recipe, not enough of an ingredient will\nproduce similar results. Of course it could also produce blander results, and in that case a potion may not be spoiled but it will simply be weak. This is preferable to exploding, of course.\nPreparation and You:\nNovice Alchemists often question me, what advice would you provide the budding alchemist, what sip of wisdom could you provide one who desires to tap your fountain of knowledge? The answer to this is rather simple, preparation is key to any potion. What\nis meant by this is that to mix a potion that you have not mixed before, or do not mix often, you must have all the facts and supplies before you begin. This is best provided in a short list of required procedures if one wishes to keep their eyebrows, \nfingers, and life.\n1. Have all the Facts\n2. Know the Recipe\n3. Clean and check all your instruments are in working order\n4. Ensure you have plenty of whatever ingredients you are using\n5. Keep and Calm Dispostion\n\nI believe the only topic that requires explanation is “Have all the Facts” and by this I mean that one should not mix a potion they know nothing about. Even having the recipe is not enough to craft a potion safely, but knowledge of its origins, \ningredients, and uses are crucial. Although budding alchemists often overlook this step, it may save your life one day.\nThe Tales of the Traveling Alchemist, Vol I\n\nCoppied by Rosso\nVaerHaven\n((13/6/13))\n", ""),
        StaticBook("Fund. Alchemy", "Rosso", "The Tales of a Travelling Alchemist\nForeword:\nthis statement is rather simple, whereas one must have a certain gift for spell-slinging, rune-craft, and spirit-speaking, one mustn’t need these frivolous commodities to use a balm to mend a wound, shroud themselves in a nigh-invisible cloak\nwith the swig from a bitter bottle, or set someone ablaze with bottled flame. Alchemy is not merely reproducing a wizard’s spell, but it is shaping the spell in a way for the common man’s use. While magic still has its place in our world, Alchemy allows\nthose without the gift to reap the same benefits as any mage might.\nChapter 1: The Fundamentals of Alchemy\nAs I was providing my services to a rather quaint hamlet, which also provided me information of the local flora and more of the same legends I always here of, I was approached by a man of common repute, and he produced a sarcastic, yet thought provoking,\nquestion. This question was, and I will attempt to reproduce the dialect as best as my phonetic ability will allow, “ ‘ow come, Mister, ‘hat ye’ can make all ‘dis ‘onderful stuff outta nothin’?” Now as shameful as his speech may be, the question remains\nthe same. How is it, that alchemists are capable or reshaping the spells of sorcerers for the common folk out of, seemingly, nothing? The answer to this question is simple to state, but fairly difficult to grasp, and this is the Principle of Equivalent\nExchange. Much like the money one gives to the merchant for the apple or slab of meat, one cannot have something for nothing. As the saying goes, “there is no such thing as a free brunch,” there is no such thing as a free potion. This fundamental rule\napplies to old alchemy, through the use of symbols and rituals, far more than what I call “Modern” alchemy. Although I will not get into ancient alchemy, I will state why this rule applies to Modern Alchemy. To craft a potion, for example a bottle of\nalchemist’s fire, one requires the materials. Much like a spell casted by a wizard, one cannot make something out of nothing. Know that to make a potion, and the possibilities are truly endless, one must take plants, objects, and other representations of \nwhat they wish to make. For example, alchemist’s fire could be best produced with an - -, a mixture of -  -, -  -, and a dash of -  -. Why would one need these items?\nThe - - provides the basic mixture of what we will make, and of course the better quality of the - - the better quality of the Alchemist’s Fire, but of course we will get further into this in a later chapter. The -  -, a -  - \nplant that grows most often in deserts, is often -  - and often associated with -  -. This provides an superb symbol for the element of -  -. The -  -, another powerful symbol of -  -, mixes well with the crushed -  -\nto produce something truly strong. The -  -, an optional portion, is another symbol of -  -, albeit both weaker than blaze -  - for both a -  - fire burns far greater than -  -.\nNow then, although you have these symbols of flame, rage, and warfare, one mustn’t simply toss objects into the metaphorical cauldron. Precision is necessary, as always. One must measure their mixture carefully, or they will give too much. And giving too\nmuch will tip the scales of Equivalent Exchange, causing unpredictable results. Why is this you might ask? When balance is achieved through Equivalent Exchanged, a mixture of the elements for the bottled spell of your desire, than all is well. But when \nyou disrupt this balance and push on the world, taking and forcing in too much of one or more element then the world will push back. Of course a word of caution is required, for one may think taking less is the best option when balance is not a certainty.\nThis is a common misconception for not providing enough of one element will result in the same conclusion, imbalance and chaos. This can be easily associated with cooking, too much of one ingredient will spoil the recipe, not enough of an ingredient will\nproduce similar results. Of course it could also produce blander results, and in that case a potion may not be spoiled but it will simply be weak. This is preferable to exploding, of course.\nPreparation and You:\nis rather simple, preparation is key to any potion. What is meant by this is that to mix a potion that you have not mixed before, or do not mix often, you must have all the facts and supplies before you begin. This is best provided in a short list \nof required procedures if one wishes to keep their eyebrows, fingers, and life.\n1. Have all the Facts\nI believe the only topic that requires explanation is “Have all the Facts” and by this I mean that one should not mix a potion they know nothing about. Even having the recipe is not enough to craft a potion safely, but knowledge of its origins, \ningredients, and uses are crucial. Although budding alchemists often overlook this step, it may save your life one day.\nThe Tales of the Traveling Alchemist, Vol I", ""),
        StaticBook("Fund. Alchemy", "Jistuma", "The Tales of a Travelling Alchemist\n------------\nThe Fundamentals of Alchemy\n\nVolume I of V\n\nForeword:\nI have heard many a man ask why one, such as myself, would take such interest and dedication in the art of alchemy when there are ready-made spells that could do just the same, if not better, a job than an apothecary’s potion. My response to\nthis statement is rather simple, whereas one must have a certain gift for spell-slinging, rune-craft, and spirit-speaking, one mustn’t need these frivolous commodities to use a balm to mend a wound, shroud themselves in a nigh-invisible cloak\nwith the swig from a bitter bottle, or set someone ablaze with bottled flame. Alchemy is not merely reproducing a wizard’s spell, but it is shaping the spell in a way for the common man’s use. While magic still has its place in our world, Alchemy allows\nthose without the gift to reap the same benefits as any mage might.\nChapter 1: The Fundamentals of Alchemy\n------------\nThe Principle and Law of Equivalent Exchange:\n\nAs I was providing my services to a rather quaint hamlet, which also provided me information of the local flora and more of the same legends I always here of, I was approached by a man of common repute, and he produced a sarcastic, yet thought provoking,\nquestion. This question was, and I will attempt to reproduce the dialect as best as my phonetic ability will allow, “ ‘ow come, Mister, ‘hat ye’ can make all ‘dis ‘onderful stuff outta nothin’?” Now as shameful as his speech may be, the question remains\nthe same. How is it, that alchemists are capable or reshaping the spells of sorcerers for the common folk out of, seemingly, nothing? The answer to this question is simple to state, but fairly difficult to grasp, and this is the Principle of Equivalent\nExchange. Much like the money one gives to the merchant for the apple or slab of meat, one cannot have something for nothing. As the saying goes, “there is no such thing as a free brunch,” there is no such thing as a free potion. This fundamental rule\napplies to old alchemy, through the use of symbols and rituals, far more than what I call “Modern” alchemy. Although I will not get into ancient alchemy, I will state why this rule applies to Modern Alchemy. To craft a potion, for example a bottle of\nalchemist’s fire, one requires the materials. Much like a spell casted by a wizard, one cannot make something out of nothing. Know that to make a potion, and the possibilities are truly endless, one must take plants, objects, and other representations of \nwhat they wish to make. For example, alchemist’s fire could be best produced with an - -, a mixture of -  -, -  -, and a dash of -  -. Why would one need these items?\nThe - - provides the basic mixture of what we will make, and of course the better quality of the - - the better quality of the Alchemist’s Fire, but of course we will get further into this in a later chapter. The -  -, a -  - \nplant that grows most often in deserts, is often -  - and often associated with -  -. This provides an superb symbol for the element of -  -. The -  -, another powerful symbol of -  -, mixes well with the crushed -  -\nto produce something truly strong. The -  -, an optional portion, is another symbol of -  -, albeit both weaker than blaze -  - for both a -  - fire burns far greater than -  -.\nNow then, although you have these symbols of flame, rage, and warfare, one mustn’t simply toss objects into the metaphorical cauldron. Precision is necessary, as always. One must measure their mixture carefully, or they will give too much. And giving too\nmuch will tip the scales of Equivalent Exchange, causing unpredictable results. Why is this you might ask? When balance is achieved through Equivalent Exchanged, a mixture of the elements for the bottled spell of your desire, than all is well. But when \nyou disrupt this balance and push on the world, taking and forcing in too much of one or more element then the world will push back. Of course a word of caution is required, for one may think taking less is the best option when balance is not a certainty.\nThis is a common misconception for not providing enough of one element will result in the same conclusion, imbalance and chaos. This can be easily associated with cooking, too much of one ingredient will spoil the recipe, not enough of an ingredient will\nproduce similar results. Of course it could also produce blander results, and in that case a potion may not be spoiled but it will simply be weak. This is preferable to exploding, of course.\nPreparation and You:\nNovice Alchemists often question me, what advice would you provide the budding alchemist, what sip of wisdom could you provide one who desires to tap your fountain of knowledge? The answer to this is rather simple, preparation is key to any potion. What\nis meant by this is that to mix a potion that you have not mixed before, or do not mix often, you must have all the facts and supplies before you begin. This is best provided in a short list of required procedures if one wishes to keep their eyebrows, \nfingers, and life.\n1. Have all the Facts\n2. Know the Recipe\n3. Clean and check all your instruments are in working order\n4. Ensure you have plenty of whatever ingredients you are using\n5. Keep and Calm Dispostion\n\nI believe the only topic that requires explanation is “Have all the Facts” and by this I mean that one should not mix a potion they know nothing about. Even having the recipe is not enough to craft a potion safely, but knowledge of its origins, \ningredients, and uses are crucial. Although budding alchemists often overlook this step, it may save your life one day.\nThe Tales of the Traveling Alchemist, Vol I\n\nCoppied by Rosso\nVaerHaven\n*Strange numbers*\n((13/6/13))\n\n      -.~Willow's~.-\n10th of Snow's Maiden, 1453: I've now travelled to southern Anthos, finding many different trees...None were right though, I knew it.\n14th of Snow's Maiden, 1453: Today I will pray to the aspects infront of the tree, then strip the branch of my choice.. I don't know what to leave.\nSycamore.~ Now I just have to fashion it to my liking...\n30th of Sun's Smile, 1453.\n1st of Deep Cold, 1453. Subject name: Rameethar\nSubject name: Zaddha\n*The writing in this book is crafted in a elegant font.*\nAa Bb Cc Dd Ee Ff Gg Hh Ii Jj Kk Ll Mm Nn Oo Pp Qq Rr Ss Tt Uu Vv Ww Xx Yy Zz\nWw Xx Yy Zz\n", ""),
        StaticBook("Funeral Invite", "Belaern Isttel", "An invitation is written to the funeral of Edmure Parkins.", ""),
        StaticBook("Game of Minds C1", "Sofetios", "           A Game of Minds\n              Chapter One\nI could make out that I was in a small stone room… and there was something else in here with me. \nI coughed and said slowly, \nAt that moment there was a slight creak to the right, as a part of the wall opened up like a door.\n I grasped his hand and he hoisted me to my feet. I nearly fell over but he helped me catch myself. I looked upwards and far above saw there was an opening to the sky.   I said,\n“They call themselves…The Followers of Truth.” He said. We got to the door and opened it. A burst of cold air came in. \n, a fireplace to the right that had a metal pan on the fire with what appeared to be a steak on it, a small vase with a small top for an opening was on the floor with a lid next to it, , a poufy cushioned chair was next to the fireplace, \nand there was also another  fireplace at the far back of the room.\n “Good. My name is Jed. And you are?”\n“The thing you should know about this place, is that it’s a death trap.” Said Jed with a serious face. He lifted his sleeve and there was a severe gash about a foot long. “That.” He said pointing to it, “Is what happens when you aren’t careful.\"\nI nodded, swallowing. “We had best get to solving this. Before the cold kills us.”  I hadn’t noticed but my toes were starting to get numb. \n“What!?” I said, “We were just in there?” \n“We are better off in here for the moment. What you need to understand is that The Followers of Truth are ruthless. If you don’t do what they want you to, they will make you do it, one way or another. Put your ear to the door.”\nI went to the door and put my ear on it like he said to, There were clicking sounds in the room. Like a giant insect walking in there.\n“Yes.” Said Jed. “A fearsome thing, they release it through the ceiling in the room to make sure we are out.” Then a thought occurred to me. I said,\n“Well If the Grimmer doesn’t come back early I guess that means we are still alive. Either way we better find the key to the door. Before they push us to find it. Now where is that key…”\n“Or to hide something else.” Said Jed. “Always test things.”  Jed took a glove off and went to the couch. I followed but at a distance. Jed, a few feet from the couch tossed it on the Seat. \n Suddenly there was a click. The cushion rose at an incredible speed to the ceiling, crushing the glove, then lowered back to position. The cushion didn’t even flatten, like it was rock hard. The glove fell on the floor.  \nJed looked to me, nodded, and said.\nI kind of whacked it with my glove. It just knocked over… and something clanked inside it. Jed looked to me and said “Be careful.” I put my glove back on, picked up the jar and looked in the opening. The key was inside it. \n“The key is in here!” I said. I turned the jar upside-down and the key came out… a little. It was stuck. \nThere was a small amount of water coming above the fireplace.  Jed cursed under his breath. I then panicked. I tried to smash the vase against the floor. “Stop!” Said Jed. The vase landed on the ground in my hand with a large thump, but didn’t break. \n“Careful you idiot!” That could have been filled with glass as well as the key.\n Jed then said after a moment,\n The cushion went back down and the vase, still whole, rolled off it. Jed cursed again. “So it’s unbreakable.” Jed then angrily threw his glove at the frosted table. As it hit the side, it stuck and turned frosted too.  I came to a sudden realization. \n“The water!” I said.\nI ran to the fireplace that was now extinguished, and filled it with water, then screwed on the lid. I turned to Jed who looked at me and looked like he just realized what I was doing and nodded. I Hurried to the table and tossed the jar at the side. \nAs it made impact it stuck, frosted, then in a few short seconds the jar exploded. Shards went everywhere. Startled, I yelled and was knocked back on my backside.  A few cut me on my arms and legs from the jar shards.   “Jed!” are you alright?” \n I heard a few curses from the back of the room.\n I picked it up and headed to the extinguished fireplace, picking up the pan.  I didn’t head to the chair to smash the ice because I didn’t want to risk breaking the key. \nBy now the water was an inch and a half deep and I couldn’t feel from my ankle down. I ran carefully to the other fireplace, which was luckily a bit raised off the ground, but not much. \n I put the pan over the fire and the ice on top of it. The pan was about 2 inches deep. Jed came behind me. After about a minute, the pan was full of water, but the ice wasn’t melted all the way. \nI took the ice in one hand and tossed the water in the pan on the ground with the other, then put the ice back on the pan and started melting it again.\nAfter another minute the key was free. I took it and went to the door where Jed was now with his ear on the door. He took his head off the door and said, \n  I unlocked it and opened it. The Grimmer wasn’t in there. I went into the room and Jed followed me, but suddenly the door closed, there was a hissing sound, like air through a tight space. I started coughing, as if the air was bad. So did Jed. \nThen everything went black.", ""),
        StaticBook("Game of Minds C1", "Sofetios Jayamen", "    A Game of Minds\n      Chapter One\nI could make out that I was in a small stone room… and there was something else in here with me. \n I could now see moderately well and saw a person sitting across from me looking straight at me. I coughed and said slowly, \n“You’re in the holding chamber, along with me.”\nAt that moment there was a slight creak to the right, as a part of the wall opened up like a door.\n“We had better get going. They don’t like it when we stall.”\n I looked upwards and far above saw there was an opening to the sky.   I said,\nWe got to the door and opened it. A burst of cold air came in.  In the room was only a table that had frost on it, A fireplace to the right that had a metal pan on the fire with what appeared to be a steak on it, \na small vase with a small top for an opening was on the floor with a lid next to it, a poufy cushioned chair was next to the fireplace, and there was also another  fireplace at the far back of the room.\n“Great…”  Said the person. “ A Puzzle.” He walked with me into the room. As we got in the door slammed behind us. “Can you stand now?” \n “Good. My name is Jed. And you are?”\nI looked to the steak and started to walk over, but as I did Jed caught my arm. “What?” I said.\nHe lifted his sleeve and there was a severe gash about a foot long. “That.” He said pointing to it, “Is what happens when you aren’t careful.”  I nodded, swallowing. “We had best get to solving this. Before the cold kills us.” \n I hadn’t noticed but my toes were starting to get numb. \n“What!?” I said, “We were just in there?” \nIf you don’t do what they want you to, they will make you do it, one way or another. Put your ear to the door.”\n“Hear that?” Said Jed. “That’s the Grimmer.”  I got my head off the door and said. “The Grimmer?”\n“Yes.” Said Jed. “A fearsome thing, they release it through the ceiling in the room to make sure we are out.” Then a thought occurred to me. I said,\n“Well If the Grimmer doesn’t come back early I guess that means we are still alive. Either way we better find the key to the door. Before they push us to find it. Now where is that key…”\n“Maybe it’s in the chair?” I said. “Seems poufy enough to hide it.”\n Jed, a few feet from the couch tossed it on the Seat. \n The cushion didn’t even flatten, like it was rock hard.  The glove fell on the floor.  Jed looked to me, nodded, and said.\nI took off my own glove went to the jar on the floor. \nI put my glove back on, picked up the jar and looked in the opening. The key was inside it. \nJed let out a small grunt. “They never make it that easy.”   Suddenly there was a trickling and hissing sound coming from the right fireplace.  There was a small amount of water coming above the fireplace.  Jed cursed under his breath.\n I then panicked. I tried to smash the vase against the floor.  “Stop!” Said Jed. The vase landed on the ground in my hand with a large thump, but didn’t break. \n“Careful you idiot!” That could have been filled with glass as well as the key.\nI forced myself to relax.  Jed then said after a moment,\n The cushion went back down and the vase, still whole, rolled off it. Jed cursed again. “So it’s unbreakable.” Jed then angrily threw his glove at the frosted table. As it hit the side, it stuck and turned frosted too.  I came to a sudden realization. \n“The water!” I said.\n I ran to the fireplace that was now extinguished, and filled it with water, then screwed on the lid. I turned to Jed who looked at me and looked like he just realized what I was doing and nodded. I Hurried to the table and tossed the jar at the side.\n As it made impact it stuck, frosted, then in a few short seconds the jar exploded. Shards went everywhere. Startled, I yelled and was knocked back on my backside.  A few cut me on my arms and legs from the jar shards.   “Jed!” are you alright?”\n I heard a few curses from the back of the room.\n I picked it up and headed to the extinguished fireplace, picking up the pan.  I didn’t head to the chair to smash the ice because I didn’t want to risk breaking the key. \nBy now the water was an inch and a half deep and I couldn’t feel from my ankle down. I ran carefully to the other fireplace, which was luckily a bit raised off the ground, but not much.\n I put the pan over the fire and the ice on top of it. The pan was about 2 inches deep. Jed came behind me. After about a minute, the pan was full of water, but the ice wasn’t melted all the way. \nI took the ice in one hand and tossed the water in the pan on the ground with the other, then put the ice back on the pan and started melting it again.\nAfter another minute the key was free. I took it and went to the door where Jed was now with his ear on the door. He took his head off the door and said,\n  I unlocked it and opened it. The Grimmer wasn’t in there. I went into the room and Jed followed me, but suddenly the door closed, there was a hissing sound, like air through a tight space. I started coughing, as if the air was bad. So did Jed. \nThen everything went black.\n~Sofetios Jayamen", ""),
        StaticBook("Game of Minds C2", "Sofetios Jayamen", "    A Game of Minds\n        Chapter Two\n “GET UP MAN!” I burst into consciousness. Jed was kneeling down in front of me with a serious worried expression. \n“I don’t know. That’s the problem! Now get up!”\n“What does that mean?” I said.\n“What do you mean?” I said now a bit panicked. Jed cursed and said,\n A loud high pitched squeal then came from somewhere above us. Jed cursed again as he ran to the wall where the door was and started banging on it. \n I was about to run forward to him but when I took the first step There was something hard beneath my shoe. I looked down. It was the key. Hurriedly I picked it up and ran to the door. Jed looked at me and then to the key. \nThere was another squeal from above. I looked up and there it was. It looked like a 6 legged thing from a nightmare. This time I cursed. I jammed the key into the small hole and turned it. \nIt opened and me and Jed went in quickly and then slammed the door shut. When we turned around, we didn’t see the furniture and things from before. We saw a person and two others beside him in black robes and hoods, which concealed their faces. \nThe middle one was clapping and smiling, he said,\n“They’re The Followers of Truth.” \n“Quiet, fool. You have no idea what that name even means.” Then the man’s head turned to me as he smiled again,\n\n“What are you talking about? My father is a merchant, not you!”  The man just laughed and said,\nThe man smiled a wicked smile then said, “Bring them in.” Suddenly the floor behind the man and the two black robed ones opened like a trapdoor. \nThree people with black robes emerged and were dragging two people with them, one was a girl that looked in her late teens, and the other was…\nWe both ran towards them but the two black robed ones with the man came forward with great speed and caught both of us with an iron grip.\n“and a father no more. Take them to the other holding chambers.” Two of the black robed people that had the girl and my father went down the trapdoor again, but the other one stayed.\n The wicked man turned to see the other black robed one there and hissed, “Didn’t you hear me? Go with them you idiot!” The black robed one that he was talking to then said,\n The black robed one extended his arms quickly toward the two ones holding Jed and I. Two large metallic needles flew out of both sleeves hitting both of the ones holding us through the neck. \nBefore Jed and I could make a move, the wicked man, startled, flew past me and Jed with amazing speed and went through the door as the man who had the needles shot one at him. \nThe door closed right before the needle hit, but the needle went right inside the keyhole sticking in it.  There was a commotion from underneath the floor where the trapdoor was.\n The person who was shooting the needles took off the black robes in a quick motion. The person had a rope around his shoulder to his waist with a grappling hook. I slightly recognized him, but I couldn’t place his face. He said,\n“Come on!” as he ran toward the door the other man went through. Jed seemed to get his wits together and said, “Come on James!”  I hesitated and said,\n“We’ll get him later, trust me, but if you want to live to see it we need to go now!” I hesitated a second then said,\n“But the Grimmer?” I said. Then I saw inside and nothing was there.\nThe man slammed the door shut and removed the needle keeping it unlocked. I looked to the man as he unraveled the grappling hook from around him, swung it around, and threw it up out of the ceiling.\n He tugged on the rope hard to make sure it was good, then started climbing up it, Jed followed, and then me. I hadn’t climbed a rope before, so I was much slower than the others. There was a banging on the door below. \nThe man had gotten to the top and Jed was nearly there as well. I was only 10 feet above the floor and it looked like I had 20 to go.  Jed got out the top and I heard the man say, “Help me pull the rope.”\n I was slowly being pulled up to the top as I still attempted to climb. I was just at the top when the door below burst open and people in black robes came in. I looked down as I grasped the side of the top. One of them had a bow and arrows.\n I was just about to get out about to my waist when I heard the sound of a bow being released. I yelled as I felt an arrow pierce my leg.\n I lost my grasp and was about to fall back into the hole, when Jed let go of the rope, dove, and grasped my arm at the last second. \nThe man ran over too and grasped my other arm, together hoisting me over the side of the hole just as another arrow flew through it. I put my arms around both their shoulders as we stood up. \nI looked around for a second, We were in what looked like an abandoned town with many large holes in the ground that looked like the one we came out of.\n“I have two horses at the entrance, let’s move!” Said the man as he coiled his rope and grappling hook rapidly. We made our way to the entrance as fast as I could go with the arrow still in my leg.\n As I looked back I saw another grappling hook come out of the hole. After half a minute of us moving and pain in my leg, we made it to the entrance. Like the man said there were two hardy looking horses tied to a post.\n I looked back again and saw a black robed arm come out of the hole.  The man got me to one of the horses and helped me on it with surprising strength. “Get on the other one.” The man said to Jed. \nJed did so as the man cut the ropes attaching the horses to the post with a large dagger that was in his belt.  He then climbed swiftly in front of me on the horse and got it moving as Jed got his moving too. \nThere was nothing coming from behind us as we got to a full gallop. I looked behind us one more time to see the people in black robes just standing at the entrance we were at. One pointed at me and made a motion of slitting his throat.\n I looked ahead of us, holding on as we rode onward.\n-by bird or in person! ", ""),
        StaticBook("Game of Minds C2", "Sofetios", "            A Game of Minds\n\n(This is going to be a continuous series of fictional books. Chapter by chapter.)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n                        -By Sofetios\n               Chapter Two\n\n                   Escape\n\nI heard a voice far away “Get up…” I struggled to get myself into consciousness. The voice came again, only a bit louder. “Get up…” Then suddenly I felt something slap me in the face. Hard. \n “GET UP MAN!” I burst into consciousness. Jed was kneeling down in front of me with a serious worried expression. \n\n“Wha- What’s going on?” I said hurriedly. Jed just said,\n\n“I don’t know. That’s the problem! Now get up!”\n\nI stood up hurriedly as Jed did too.  Jed looked to the stone wall and said, “The door, it hasn’t opened yet.” \n\n“What does that mean?” I said.\n\n“It means that if we don’t get in there soon, we’re dead.” Jed said as he pointed up. \nI looked up to the opening in the top. I saw the sun blaring down at me.\n\n“What do you mean?” I said now a bit panicked. Jed cursed and said,\n\n“The Grimmer! That’s what I mean! The door always opens by noon!” \n A loud high pitched squeal then came from somewhere above us. Jed cursed again as he ran to the wall where the door was and started banging on it. \n\n“Let us in you cultists!”\n I was about to run forward to him but when I took the first step There was something hard beneath my shoe. I looked down. It was the key. Hurriedly I picked it up and ran to the door. Jed looked at me and then to the key. \nThere was another squeal from above. I looked up and there it was. It looked like a 6 legged thing from a nightmare. This time I cursed. I jammed the key into the small hole and turned it. \nIt opened and me and Jed went in quickly and then slammed the door shut. When we turned around, we didn’t see the furniture and things from before. We saw a person and two others beside him in black robes and hoods, which concealed their faces. \nThe middle one was clapping and smiling, he said,\n\n“Well done, well done. I didn’t think you would make it through that last one, James.”\n\n“Who are you?” I said. Then Jed said slowly,\n\n“They’re The Followers of Truth.”  \n“Quite, fool. You have no idea what that name even means.” Then the man’s head turned to me as he smiled again, \n “Now James, don’t you recognize me? Or maybe you wouldn’t seeing as we’ve never seen each other directly. I am your father.” I was taken aback heavily at this remark as Jed looked at me. I said,\n“What are you talking about? My father is a merchant, not you!”  The man just laughed and said,\n\n“Your ‘father’ was just a tool. Did he never tell you how he got you? Oh the things people tell their kids. Such a shame.” \nThe man smiled a wicked smile then said, “Bring them in.” Suddenly the floor behind the man and the two black robed ones opened like a trapdoor. \nThree people with black robes emerged and were dragging two people with them, one was a girl that looked in her late teens, and the other was…\n\n“Father!” I yelled. \n\n“Lily!” yelled Jed next to me. \nWe both ran towards them but the two black robed ones with the man came forward with great speed and caught both of us with an iron grip.\n\n “Yes, Father indeed.” Said the man that was in the middle with a chuckle. \n“and a father no more. Take them to the other holding chambers.” Two of the black robed people that had the girl and my father went down the trapdoor again, but the other one stayed. \n The wicked man turned to see the other black robed one there and hissed, “Didn’t you hear me? Go with them you idiot!” The black robed one that he was talking to then said,\n\n“Not today.” \n The black robed one extended his arms quickly toward the two ones holding Jed and I. Two large metallic needles flew out of both sleeves hitting both of the ones holding us through the neck. \nBefore Jed and I could make a move, the wicked man, startled, flew past me and Jed with amazing speed and went through the door as the man who had the needles shot one at him. \nThe door closed right before the needle hit, but the needle went right inside the keyhole sticking in it.  There was a commotion from underneath the floor where the trapdoor was. \n The person who was shooting the needles took off the black robes in a quick motion. The person had a rope around his shoulder to his waist with a grappling hook. I slightly recognized him, but I couldn’t place his face. He said,\n“Come on!” as he ran toward the door the other man went through. Jed seemed to get his wits together and said, “Come on James!”  I hesitated and said,\n\n“But my father!” The man who was in the black robes then said,\n\n“We’ll get him later, trust me, but if you want to live to see it we need to go now!” I hesitated a second then said,\n\n“Fine. How do we get out?”\n\n“Through the door.” Said the man opening it.\n\n“But the Grimmer?” I said. Then I saw inside and nothing was there.\n\n“He used it to escape.” Said the man, “It’s his pet. Now come on!”\n\nJed and I hurried through the door after him. \nThe man slammed the door shut and removed the needle keeping it unlocked. I looked to the man as he unraveled the grappling hook from around him, swung it around, and threw it up out of the ceiling. \nHe tugged on the rope hard to make sure it was good, then started climbing up it, Jed followed, and then me. I hadn’t climbed a rope before, so I was much slower than the others. There was a banging on the door below. \nThe man had gotten to the top and Jed was nearly there as well. I was only 10 feet above the floor and it looked like I had 20 to go.  Jed got out the top and I heard the man say, “Help me pull the rope.”\n I was slowly being pulled up to the top as I still attempted to climb. I was just at the top when the door below burst open and people in black robes came in. I looked down as I grasped the side of the top. One of them had a bow and arrows. \n I was just about to get out about to my waist when I heard the sound of a bow being released. I yelled as I felt an arrow pierce my leg. \n I lost my grasp and was about to fall back into the hole, when Jed let go of the rope, dove, and grasped my arm at the last second. \nThe man ran over too and grasped my other arm, together hoisting me over the side of the hole just as another arrow flew through it. I put my arms around both their shoulders as we stood up. \nI looked around for a second, We were in what looked like an abandoned town with many large holes in the ground that looked like the one we came out of.\n“I have two horses at the entrance, let’s move!” Said the man as he coiled his rope and grappling hook rapidly. We made our way to the entrance as fast as I could go with the arrow still in my leg. \n As I looked back I saw another grappling hook come out of the hole. After half a minute of us moving and pain in my leg, we made it to the entrance. Like the man said there were two hardy looking horses tied to a post. \nI looked back again and saw a black robed arm come out of the hole.  The man got me to one of the horses and helped me on it with surprising strength. “Get on the other one.” The man said to Jed. \nJed did so as the man cut the ropes attaching the horses to the post with a large dagger that was in his belt.  He then climbed swiftly in front of me on the horse and got it moving as Jed got his moving too. \nThere was nothing coming from behind us as we got to a full gallop. I looked behind us one more time to see the people in black robes just standing at the entrance we were at. One pointed at me and made a motion of slitting his throat. \n I looked ahead of us, holding on as we rode onward.\n\n(End of Chapter Two)\n\nIf you like my writing, please send me a bird saying so. ((Sofetios)) I’ll be writing more in the future!\n\n-Sofetios\n\nRegen V.2 (10 Sec)", ""),
        StaticBook("Game of Minds C3", "Sofetios", "          A Game of Minds\n             Chapter Three\nThe man in front of me noticed my shift in weight and looked at the arrow to for a second. Then he cursed, looked over to Jed who was riding beside us and said, “Stop the horse!” Both Jed’s horse and the horse I was on came to a stop. \nThe man got off and motioned me to get off. I got off as he helped me down and Jed got off his as well.\n“Exactly why we need to stop.” Said the man. He took the horses rein with one hand and helped me walk with the other. Jed followed behind us with his horse as we went into the thick woods on the side of the path we were on. \nWe were walking in the woods for a bit when the man said, “Do you feel cold? Weak as well?”\n“No, not this fast at least.”  I looked at him and said, \n“The blood already clotting on your leg isn’t a good thing, It also isn’t just a coincidence. The clotting allows the blood to circulate unhindered.” I put the pieces of information together, then said,\n“Poisoned, yes.” Said the man seriously. I panicked and reached down to the arrow to grasp it and pull it out, but the man grabbed my hand before I could do it. “It’s best to leave it in for now, trust me.” \n“What are we going to do!?” I half said half yelled. The man didn’t say anything for a few seconds then said,\nWe were still walking in the woods until I spotted a wooden hut with a straw roof. By now I was freezing and stumbling even though it was sunny out and I had the mans support. The man tied both horses to a tree then we went toward the hut. \n We came to its entrance and the man peeked inside. There was no one there.  “Era?” The man said. “You in here?” We walked a bit into the hut on the wooden floor until there was a crunch of paper under the man’s foot. \n We came to its entrance and the man peeked inside. There was no one there.  “Era?” The man said. “You in here?” We walked a bit into the hut on the wooden floor until there was a crunch of paper under the man’s foot. \n“MOVE!” The man yelled. He basically ran and dragged me out at the same time. Jed was right behind us. We were about 30 feet from the hut when I felt heat from behind me. We slowed down, stopped, and turned around. The hut was in flames. \nI turned to the man and saw he had fear and anger in his eyes. He looked quickly at the paper. Then he jammed it into a ball, yelled, and threw it at the fire. The man was breathing heavily in anger, Then as he started to calm down,\nhe said in a lower voice, “They have her…”  Neither Jed or I said anything, Until the man turned his head a bit to me and said, “I’m sorry, but you are most likely going to pass out with the poison.”\n“You mean I’m not going to die?” I said.\n“There’s got to be another way to get something to cure the poison.” The man shook his head and said,\n“Then lets go!” Said Jed, “We can put James on the horse, get out of here, and find someone else to give the cure!”\n“We’re most likely surrounded, or soon to be. That trap wasn’t meant to kill us, it was meant as a signal.” These words made me feel more chilled then the poison did. I turned to the hut once again. Then, I saw it. \nThe hut had burnt to the ground, but also the wooden floor that was there, what was left was the outline of a square on stone. \n“Could it…” He started saying but never finished. “Let’s move!” We went as fast as we could toward the remains but I could barely walk. When we got there, the man pulled out his long dagger and fit it between the crack where the outline was. \nThen used it like a lever lifting the square indent. As it rose I got a strong smell of herbs coming from underneath. The man held the stone square up and told Jed to help me down. There was a steep stepping ladder going down.\nAs I descended with Jed’s help I noticed bottles and beakers in the faint lighting as well as their shadows against the wooden plank walls. There were also cabinets against the wall as well as a large wooden bookcase, except there were beakers on it. \nWhen we got to the floor, the man came after us. Jed sat me down on an old wooden chair that was there as the man went though cabinets and bottle looking for the right one.  \n The man ran to me carrying a small bottle, a piece of cloth, a leaf, and a piece of leather. He opened the bottle and whispered, “Brace yourself, this will hurt. It is very important you don’t make noise.” He gave me the piece of leather and said, \n“Bite on this.” I did so. He grasped the arrow and carefully but quickly took it out, along with a bit of the clotted blood. I grunted in pain and bit down hard on the leather as it was removed and the blood started rushing out. \nHe then quickly poured some of the liquid in the bottle on the wound. It felt like lava as it fizzed and bubbled. I felt like I was getting stronger and warmer at the same time but it was still painful. \nThen the man after pouring more on quickly put the leaf on the wound and wrapped the cloth around it, tying it in place. As soon as the leaf made contact with the wound I felt a rushing feeling of relief, as if the leaf was absorbing the pain.  \n  After the man was done, he whispered,  “That should do it. Now we better get out of here and fast. Can you stand?” I tried standing up, I was a bit wobbly but much better than before. “Good.” whispered the man to Jed and I. \n“They are probably waiting above us as we speak. Now our only chance is to find an alternative exit. Era would most likely have made a side tunnel down here. Let’s get to looking.”  We then dispersed around the room quietly. \nJed was checking large cabinets, as the man was checking the floor by knocking on different parts of it. I decided to do the same thing with the walls. I went all around the room and all the wood planks had the same sound when I knocked on them.\nI looked to the other two, the man was shaking his head as he kept knocking on the floor, and Jed was making his way to the bookcase, until he tripped. Jed reached out attempting to grab something to catch his fall. He ended up grabbing an empty beaker. \nInstead of him crashing to the floor and breaking the beaker, The beaker stayed on the shelve, as if it was attached somehow. Jed gathered his balance and stood up looking at the beaker. Then man came over and I did as well. Jed then whispered,\n“Who makes a beaker that won’t come off?”\nJed and the man carefully lifted the bookcase and carried it to the side dropping it slowly. Behind the bookshelf was… more planks. The man went to them and knocked on one. \n It sounded the same as the others. He cursed and looked like he was thinking hard. Then I noticed something, I looked up and there was a tiny beam of light coming from the top of one of the planks. I whispered,\n“Look.” To the man. He looked up where I was looking and whispered slowly,\n He wiggled the blade hard and the planks shifted slightly to the side. Both the man and I looked into the crack it created. The planks were about a foot thick, and on the other side there was what looked like a tunnel. “No wonder…” he whispered. \n“Get me the chair you were sitting in James.” I went over to the chair and brought it over to him. He took it and placed it against the planks to the left of the one that had the light, out of its way. \nHe took out his dagger and pulled the plank toward where it was cracked to make a small bit of space on the bottom of the plank. He put his dagger in the middle of the bottom space so the plank was balanced on it. \nThen he put his hands under the plank and moved it the plank out a bit, but then it got stuck. He whispered a curse then he whispered to me, \n“Now I know your still weak James, but I need you to get on this chair and pull the top of the plank, Jed and I will catch it so we don’t make too much noise if it falls. And here use this to get enough of the plank exposed to pull.” \n He pulled out a second dagger from his belt and held it out. I nodded and took the dagger. Then the man nodded and I got up on the chair. I put the dagger in-between where the plank met the ceiling. \nI wiggled it up and down until there was about an inch of the plank exposed. The man looked up and whispered “Now pull.” I pulled with all of my strength and so did the man. \n There was a slight scraping sound of the dagger being dragged with the plank across the ground. Jed grasped the plank and helped too when it was out enough. \n As soon as it got free from the wall, the man quickly took out the dagger on the bottom so the plank didn’t lean one way and topple over. “Alright.” He whispered. “Now to lean it down, slowly.” \n The man, Jed, and I slowly leaned the heavy plank down on the floor so it didn’t make much of a sound. When we finished, we looked through where the plank was. There was a stone passageway and on the side of the left wall was a lit torch.\nThe man went through the opening and Jed and I followed. He went up to the torch and whispered,\nWe then headed down the passage.", ""),
        StaticBook("Game of Minds C3", "Sofetios Jayamen", "   A Game of Minds\n      Chapter Three\nThe man in front of me noticed my shift in weight and looked at the arrow to for a second. Then he cursed, looked over to Jed who was riding beside us and said, “Stop the horse!” Both Jed’s horse and the horse I was on came to a stop.\n The man got off and motioned me to get off. I got off as he helped me down and Jed got off his as well.\n“The arrow can wait!” Said Jed. “It’s already clotted over anyways.”\n Jed followed behind us with his horse as we went into the thick woods on the side of the path we were on.  We were walking in the woods for a bit when the man said, “Do you feel cold? Weak as well?”\n“Actually yes.” I said a bit worried. “Isn’t that normal for an arrow wound?” The man cursed under his breath then said,\n“What do you mean?” The man sighed and said,\nI put the pieces of information together, then said,\n I panicked and reached down to the arrow to grasp it and pull it out, but the man grabbed my hand before I could do it.\n“What are we going to do!?” I half said half yelled. The man didn’t say anything for a few seconds then said,\nWe were still walking in the woods until I spotted a wooden hut with a straw roof. By now I was freezing and stumbling even though it was sunny out and I had the mans support. \nThe man tied both horses to a tree then we went toward the hut. \n We walked a bit into the hut on the wooden floor until there was a crunch of paper under the man’s foot. \nA piece of the paper was torn off by something.  Then I saw it.  It was being dragged fast by a small piece of string. \nJed was right behind us. We were about 30 feet from the hut when I felt heat from behind me. We slowed down, stopped, and turned around. The hut was in flames.\n He looked quickly at the paper. Then he jammed it into a ball, yelled, and threw it at the fire. The man was breathing heavily in anger, Then as he started to calm down,  he said in a lower voice, “They have her…”  Neither Jed or I said anything,\n Until the man turned his head a bit to me and said, “I’m sorry, but you are most likely going to pass out with the poison.”\n“No, you’re not.” Said the man, “He, wants you alive.” For some reason I felt that passing out would be worse than dying. \n“The only other place that would have the poison’s cure is Era’s Stash. Even I’ve never found it.” The hut was nearly burnt to the ground now.\n“Then lets go!” Said Jed, “We can put James on the horse, get out of here, and find someone else to give the cure!”\n“We’re most likely surrounded, or soon to be. That trap wasn’t meant to kill us, it was meant as a signal.” These words made me feel more chilled then the poison did. I turned to the hut once again. Then, I saw it. \nThe hut had burnt to the ground, but also the wooden floor that was there, what was left was the outline of a square on stone. \n“Could it…” He started saying but never finished. “Let’s move!” We went as fast as we could toward the remains but I could barely walk. When we got there, the man pulled out his long dagger and fit it between the crack where the outline was. \nThen used it like a lever lifting the square indent. As it rose I got a strong smell of herbs coming from underneath. The man held the stone square up and told Jed to help me down. There was a steep stepping ladder going down.\nAs I descended with Jed’s help I noticed bottles and beakers in the faint lighting as well as their shadows against the wooden plank walls. There were also cabinets against the wall as well as a large wooden bookcase, except there were beakers on it. \nWhen we got to the floor, the man came after us. Jed sat me down on an old wooden chair that was there as the man went though cabinets and bottle looking for the right one. \n The man ran to me carrying a small bottle, a piece of cloth, a leaf, and a piece of leather. He opened the bottle and whispered, “Brace yourself, this will hurt. It is very important you don’t make noise.” He gave me the piece of leather and said, \n“Bite on this.” I did so. He grasped the arrow and carefully but quickly took it out, along with a bit of the clotted blood. I grunted in pain and bit down hard on the leather as it was removed and the blood started rushing out. \nHe then quickly poured some of the liquid in the bottle on the wound. It felt like lava as it fizzed and bubbled. I felt like I was getting stronger and warmer at the same time but it was still painful.\n Then the man after pouring more on quickly put the leaf on the wound and wrapped the cloth around it, tying it in place. As soon as the leaf made contact with the wound I felt a rushing feeling of relief, as if the leaf was absorbing the pain.\n  After the man was done, he whispered,  “That should do it. Now we better get out of here and fast. Can you stand?” I tried standing up, I was a bit wobbly but much better than before. “Good.” whispered the man to Jed and I. \n“They are probably waiting above us as we speak. Now our only chance is to find an alternative exit. Era would most likely have made a side tunnel down here. Let’s get to looking.”  We then dispersed around the room quietly. \nThank you for taking the time to read this. I'll be writing more in the future!", ""),
        StaticBook("Gate Report", "Emerson Bilokir'Tayilu", "[!]*The page lists off the entree and departure times, dates, and the ammount of visitors/citizens leaving at the time*\n1 Departing\nUnknown potions:\npoppy + dandilion + acacia sapling:\nwheat + flesh + bean+ spider eye + poppy:\nfeather:\nsnowball + spider eye+ suger cane: Bungling potion.\nBrick + suger:\npotions and what they do:\nroots slowly coming out: poppy:", ""),
