        StaticBook("L.F.B.", "Chumpchump", "       Lavada Family Brewery\n---------------------------------------------------The Goldman Company shall provide the Lavada Family Brewery with advertising throughout all Goldman Co. franchised taverns in Anthos.\n\nIn return, L.F.B. shall return 15% of\ncompany revenues towards the Goldman Company as well as 1 keg of ale each elf week for every Goldman Co. franchised tavern.\n\n\nTerms & Conditions:\nThis contract may be re-negotiated 2 elf months time from this day (6/19).\n\nGoldman Co. may terminate this \ncontract at any point.\n\nSigned:\n\nX~~Toveah Goldman\n\nX~~Lavada Family Brewery", ""),
        StaticBook("Lady in White", "Garth Hightower", "\n   The Lady in White\n\n\n  There she stood, the Lady in White, radiant in the midsummer sun.\n  O never since that first gaze have I witnessed such a face so beautiful and comparable to none.\n  Her deep red eyes\nlike priceless rubies gazed out over the ocean with green waves like grass, strewn with white. \n  From afar I watched this beautiful sight, her light yellow hair blowing in the wind like fields of silk and golden wheat.\n  O how my heart yearned at the\nsight of such magestic beauty, a heart of lust turned to adoration which to thee is quite a feat.\n  Alas the sun began to set and rays like glass set upon the shore, she turned and departed silver crown and all.\n  And I looked down in sorrowful defeat,\nknowing to see such a sight again would be a near impossible treat.\n  But do not give thee up hope, as the scene did prove, that truly rare sights may indeed be seen by lovers eyes.\n  For on that day I saw a Lady in White, pained and betrayed but so full \nof love, that my heart did yearn to with her rise, like a springtime flower reaching towards sunny skies.\n  Together and forever we may one day reside. In mutual love that would never die.\n\n- Hath Roger Wight", ""),
        StaticBook("Lady Sophia", "Oracle", "The Legend of Lady Sophia\nOn the island called Silva Insula*, there is a legend** of a Sainted woman named Sophie...and a dark evil.\nThey would leave the inn, or step out of their homes for a minute and just vanish, never to be seen again. Then people noticed that some of the others were slowly changing, becoming wilder and more aggressive, or just insane.\nPeople became frightened and began to withdraw from each other, joining with their neighbors in an effort to stay safe. Lady Sophie and her son Trevor were among those who sought shelter and safety at the inn.\nAround that time creatures began to appear around the town. No two descriptions were ever the same, but most could agree on their glowing eyes and how they seemed to fade into the darkness around them when you looked at them.\nThen Trevor went insane overnight. He began to kill his friends and neighbors. People from the small societies banded together and tried many ways to stop him, but, alas, the only way for his terror to end was by his death.\nOn the day he was buried, the Lady Sophie sat all day by her beloved son's grave, neither eating nor speaking to any who came by to offer her their condolences. The remaining people gathered at the Inn to wait for her return.\nBy the time night fell, a dwarf named Brokk became concerned for her and went to the grave to check on her. As he approached the grave, Lady Sophie stood up and walked out into the misty forest. Brokk tried to follow her but quickly lost\nher and turned to go back to the inn. That's when the scream pierced through the fog and darkness. No one could find the words to describe that unearthly, chill inducing scream. Many recalled grabbing their heads from the pain as the scream ripped\nthroughout their brains. When the scream ended, Brokk went in the direction Lady Sophie went in, and found her unconscious on the ground. He picked her up in his arms and took her back to the inn.\nLady Sophie woke once and tried to tell Brokk what happened. She had found the source of the evil plaguing them and went to confront it. The scream that had rent the air had come from this evil as she defeated it. **** She only had time to\nmention that it would never return before she closed her eyes and passed into that unknown realm we call death. As she died, all the others who had been slipping into madness, became whole once more. No one else disappeared after that. Life went\nback to normal for the people of Silva Insula. They moved closer to the inn and resurrected a church in her honor; it still stands to this day watching over her beloved town.\none theory that many in the town seemed accept as fact. They said that Lady Sophie, after grieving for her loss so deeply, became determined that no one else should suffer her fate. It is whispered that her will and determinations\nwas so strong, that she defeated the evil with her mind alone. And some even whisper that the evil that she faced was somehow connected to the enders that plague Asulon now.\nnoises coming from the graveyard late at night and the shadow that rises from Trevor's grave and prowls around the town looking for a new victim.\n-footnotes-\n**The tale has been passed from a local Dwarf named Brokk Wayland MacGowan to his brother Ferron Andvare MacGowan and the townspeople who were left by the end.\nLucky Goblin Inn.He is considered to be the founder of Silva Insula and all it stands for.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lady Sophia", "Leyu", "The Legend of Lady Sophia\nDiscovered and Recovered by Talithia\nProtected by the Monks of Asulon\nRetold by Leyu-Meahr\nVaelhaven\n*Strange Numbers\n((9/19/13))\nOn the island called Silva Insula*, there is a legend** of a Sainted woman named Sophie...and a dark evil.\nIt all began when people who lived small societies near the Lucky Goblin Inn began to go missing.\nThey would leave the inn, or step out of their homes for a minute and just vanish, never to be seen again. Then people noticed that some of the others were slowly changing, becoming wilder and more aggressive, or just insane.\nPeople became frightened and began to withdraw from each other, joining with their neighbors in an effort to stay safe. Lady Sophie and her son Trevor were among those who sought shelter and safety at the inn.\nAround that time creatures began to appear around the town. No two descriptions were ever the same, but most could agree on their glowing eyes and how they seemed to fade into the darkness around them when you looked at them.\nThen Trevor went insane overnight. He began to kill his friends and neighbors. People from the small societies banded together and tried many ways to stop him, but, alas, the only way for his terror to end was by his death.\nOn the day he was buried, the Lady Sophie sat all day by her beloved son's grave, neither eating nor speaking to any who came by to offer her their condolences. The remaining people gathered at the Inn to wait for her return.\nBy the time night fell, a dwarf named Brokk became concerned for her and went to the grave to check on her. As he approached the grave, Lady Sophie stood up and walked out into the misty forest. Brokk tried to follow her but quickly lost\nher and turned to go back to the inn. That's when the scream pierced through the fog and darkness. No one could find the words to describe that unearthly, chill inducing scream. Many recalled grabbing their heads from the pain as the scream ripped\nthroughout their brains. When the scream ended, Brokk went in the direction Lady Sophie went in, and found her unconscious on the ground. He picked her up in his arms and took her back to the inn.\nAs the sun banished the darkness,\nLady Sophie woke once and tried to tell Brokk what happened. She had found the source of the evil plaguing them and went to confront it. The scream that had rent the air had come from this evil as she defeated it. **** She only had time to\nmention that it would never return before she closed her eyes and passed into that unknown realm we call death. As she died, all the others who had been slipping into madness, became whole once more. No one else disappeared after that. Life went\nback to normal for the people of Silva Insula. They moved closer to the inn and resurrected a church in her honor; it still stands to this day watching over her beloved town.\nAlthough none knew what actually happened that night, there is\none theory that many in the town seemed accept as fact. They said that Lady Sophie, after grieving for her loss so deeply, became determined that no one else should suffer her fate. It is whispered that her will and determinations\nwas so strong, that she defeated the evil with her mind alone. And some even whisper that the evil that she faced was somehow connected to the enders that plague Asulon now.\nBut perhaps the most disturbing thing of all is the rumors of\nnoises coming from the graveyard late at night and the shadow that rises from Trevor's grave and prowls around the town looking for a new victim.\n-footnotes-\n*Silva Insula is known for the Lucky Goblin's Inn and it's delicious Red Rum, which is no longer made. There are several abandoned villages near Silva Insula that date back to the events described in this legend\n**The tale has been passed from a local Dwarf named Brokk Wayland MacGowan to his brother Ferron Andvare MacGowan and the townspeople who were left by the end.\n*** Brokk Wayland MacGowan is the builder and original owner of the\nLucky Goblin Inn.He is considered to be the founder of Silva Insula and all it stands for.\n****The means to defeating this unknown evil was either lost or Lady Sophie never said\nwhat she did to defeat it.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lady Sophia", "Domainoft", "The Legend of Lady Sophia\nDiscovered and Recovered by Talithia\nProtected by the Monks of Asulon\nRecounted by Leyu-Meahr\nVaelhaven\n*Strange Numbers\n((3/3/13))\nOn the island called Silva Insula*, there is a legend** of a Sainted woman named Sophie...and a dark evil.\nIt all began when people who lived small societies near the Lucky Goblin Inn began to go missing.\nThey would leave the inn, or step out of their homes for a minute and just vanish, never to be seen again. Then people noticed that some of the others were slowly changing, becoming wilder and more aggressive, or just insane.\nPeople became frightened and began to withdraw from each other, joining with their neighbors in an effort to stay safe. Lady Sophie and her son Trevor were among those who sought shelter and safety at the inn.\nAround that time creatures began to appear around the town. No two descriptions were ever the same, but most could agree on their glowing eyes and how they seemed to fade into the darkness around them when you looked at them.\nThen Trevor went insane overnight. He began to kill his friends and neighbors. People from the small societies banded together and tried many ways to stop him, but, alas, the only way for his terror to end was by his death.\nOn the day he was buried, the Lady Sophie sat all day by her beloved son's grave, neither eating nor speaking to any who came by to offer her their condolences. The remaining people gathered at the Inn to wait for her return.\nBy the time night fell, a dwarf named Brokk became concerned for her and went to the grave to check on her. As he approached the grave, Lady Sophie stood up and walked out into the misty forest. Brokk tried to follow her but quickly lost\nher and turned to go back to the inn. That's when the scream pierced through the fog and darkness. No one could find the words to describe that unearthly, chill inducing scream. Many recalled grabbing their heads from the pain as the scream ripped\nthroughout their brains. When the scream ended, Brokk went in the direction Lady Sophie went in, and found her unconscious on the ground. He picked her up in his arms and took her back to the inn.\nAs the sun banished the darkness,\nLady Sophie woke once and tried to tell Brokk what happened. She had found the source of the evil plaguing them and went to confront it. The scream that had rent the air had come from this evil as she defeated it. **** She only had time to\nmention that it would never return before she closed her eyes and passed into that unknown realm we call death. As she died, all the others who had been slipping into madness, became whole once more. No one else disappeared after that. Life went\nback to normal for the people of Silva Insula. They moved closer to the inn and resurrected a church in her honor; it still stands to this day watching over her beloved town.\nAlthough none knew what actually happened that night, there is\none theory that many in the town seemed accept as fact. They said that Lady Sophie, after grieving for her loss so deeply, became determined that no one else should suffer her fate. It is whispered that her will and determinations\nwas so strong, that she defeated the evil with her mind alone. And some even whisper that the evil that she faced was somehow connected to the enders that plague Asulon now.\nBut perhaps the most disturbing thing of all is the rumors of\nnoises coming from the graveyard late at night and the shadow that rises from Trevor's grave and prowls around the town looking for a new victim.\n-footnotes-\n*Silva Insula is known for the Lucky Goblin's Inn and it's delicious Red Rum, which is no longer made. There are several abandoned villages near Silva Insula that date back to the events described in this legend\n**The tale has been passed from a local Dwarf named Brokk Wayland MacGowan to his brother Ferron Andvare MacGowan and the townspeople who were left by the end.\n*** Brokk Wayland MacGowan is the builder and original owner of the\nLucky Goblin Inn.He is considered to be the founder of Silva Insula and all it stands for.\n****The means to defeating this unknown evil was either lost or Lady Sophie never said\nwhat she did to defeat it.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lady Sophie", "Jistuma", "\nOn the island called Silva Insula*, there is a legend** of a Sainted woman named Sophie...and a dark evil.\nIt all began when people who lived in small societies near the Lucky Goblin Inn began to go missing. They would leave the inn, or step out of their homes for a minute and just vanish, never to be seen again. Then people noticed that some of the others\nwere slowly changing, becoming wilder and more aggressive, or just insane. People became frightened and began to withdraw from each other, joining with their neighbors in an effort to stay safe. Lady Sophie and her son Trevor were among those who sought\nshelter and safety at the inn. Around that time creatures began to appear around the town. No two descriptions were ever the same, but most could agree on their glowing eyes and how they seemed to fade into the darkness around them when you looked at them\nThen Trevor went insane overnight. He began to kill his friends and neighbors. People from the small societies banded together and tried many ways to stop him, but, alas, the only way for his terror to end was by his death.\nOn the day he was buried, the Lady Sophie sat all day by her beloved son's grave, neither eating nor speaking to any who came by to offer her their condolences. The remaining people gathered at the Inn to wait for her return. By the time night fell, a\ndwarf named Brokk*** became concerned for her and went to the grave to check on her. As he approached the grave, Lady Sophie stood up and walked out into the misty forest. Brokk tried to follow her but quickly lost her and turned to go back to the inn.\nThat's when the scream pierced through the fog and darkness. No one could find the words to describe that unearthly, chill inducing scream. Many recalled grabbing their heads from the pain as the scream ripped throughout their brains. When the\nscream ended, Brokk went in the direction Lady Sophie went in, and found her unconscious on the ground. He picked her up in his arms and took her back to the inn.\nAs the sun banished the darkness, Lady Sophie woke once and tried to tell Brokk what happened. She had found the source of the evil plaguing them and went to confront it. The scream that had rent the air had come from this evil as she defeated it. **** \nShe only had time to mention that it would never return before she closed her eyes and passed into that unknown realm we call death. As she died, all the others who had been slipping into madness, became whole once more. No one else disappeared after\nthat. Life went back to normal for the people of Silva Insula. They moved closer to the inn and resurrected a church in her honor; it still stands to this day watching over her beloved town.\nAlthough none knew what actually happened that night, there is one theory that many in the town seemed accept as fact. They said that Lady Sophie, after grieving for her loss so deeply, became determined that no one else should suffer her fate. It is\nwhispered that her will and determinations was so strong, that she defeated the evil with her mind alone. And some even whisper that the evil that she faced was somehow connected to the enders that plague Asulon now.\nBut perhaps the most disturbing thing of all is the rumors of noises coming from the graveyard late at night and the shadow that rises from Trevor's grave and prowls around the town looking for a new victim.\n-footnotes-\n**The tale has been passed from a local Dwarf named Brokk Wayland MacGowan to his brother Ferron Andvare MacGowan and the townspeople who were left by the end.\n*** Brokk Wayland MacGowan is the builder and original owner of the Lucky Goblin Inn. He is considered to be the founder of Silva Insula and all it stands for.\n****The means to defeating this unknown evil was either lost or Lady Sophie never said what she did to defeat it.\nThe Legend of Lady Sophie\nBaile intelligence report Volume II\nRoster of Rangers\nNew blood Aryton\nMost of the rangers will probably remain loyal no matter the bribe. I doubt that the information about the privy council will change anything but i'll continue on from my last report on the next page", ""),
        StaticBook("Lady Sophie", "Rosso", "The Legend of Lady Sophia\nOn the island called Silva Insula*, there is a legend** of a Sainted woman named Sophie...and a dark evil.\nIt all began when people who lived in small societies near the Lucky Goblin Inn began to go missing. They would leave the inn, or step out of their homes for a minute and just vanish, never to be seen again. Then people noticed that some of the others\nwere slowly changing, becoming wilder and more aggressive, or just insane. People became frightened and began to withdraw from each other, joining with their neighbors in an effort to stay safe. Lady Sophie and her son Trevor were among those who sought\nshelter and safety at the inn. Around that time creatures began to appear around the town. No two descriptions were ever the same, but most could agree on their glowing eyes and how they seemed to fade into the darkness around them when you looked at them\nThen Trevor went insane overnight. He began to kill his friends and neighbors. People from the small societies banded together and tried many ways to stop him, but, alas, the only way for his terror to end was by his death.\nOn the day he was buried, the Lady Sophie sat all day by her beloved son's grave, neither eating nor speaking to any who came by to offer her their condolences. The remaining people gathered at the Inn to wait for her return. By the time night fell, a\ndwarf named Brokk*** became concerned for her and went to the grave to check on her. As he approached the grave, Lady Sophie stood up and walked out into the misty forest. Brokk tried to follow her but quickly lost her and turned to go back to the inn.\nThat's when the scream pierced through the fog and darkness. No one could find the words to describe that unearthly, chill inducing scream. Many recalled grabbing their heads from the pain as the scream ripped throughout their brains. When the\nscream ended, Brokk went in the direction Lady Sophie went in, and found her unconscious on the ground. He picked her up in his arms and took her back to the inn.\nAs the sun banished the darkness, Lady Sophie woke once and tried to tell Brokk what happened. She had found the source of the evil plaguing them and went to confront it. The scream that had rent the air had come from this evil as she defeated it. **** \nShe only had time to mention that it would never return before she closed her eyes and passed into that unknown realm we call death. As she died, all the others who had been slipping into madness, became whole once more. No one else disappeared after\nthat. Life went back to normal for the people of Silva Insula. They moved closer to the inn and resurrected a church in her honor; it still stands to this day watching over her beloved town.\nAlthough none knew what actually happened that night, there is one theory that many in the town seemed accept as fact. They said that Lady Sophie, after grieving for her loss so deeply, became determined that no one else should suffer her fate. It is\nwhispered that her will and determinations was so strong, that she defeated the evil with her mind alone. And some even whisper that the evil that she faced was somehow connected to the enders that plague Asulon now.\nBut perhaps the most disturbing thing of all is the rumors of noises coming from the graveyard late at night and the shadow that rises from Trevor's grave and prowls around the town looking for a new victim.\n-footnotes-\n\n*Silva Insula is known for the Lucky Goblin's Inn and it's delicious Red Rum, which is no longer made. There are several abandoned villages near Silva Insula that date back to the events described in this legend\n**The tale has been passed from a local Dwarf named Brokk Wayland MacGowan to his brother Ferron Andvare MacGowan and the townspeople who were left by the end.\n*** Brokk Wayland MacGowan is the builder and original owner of the Lucky Goblin Inn. He is considered to be the founder of Silva Insula and all it stands for.\n****The means to defeating this unknown evil was either lost or Lady Sophie never said what she did to defeat it.\nThe Legend of Lady Shopie\n\nDiscovered and Recovered by Talithia\nCoppied by Rosso\nVaerHaven\n", ""),
        StaticBook("Laenry's Will", "ilikefooddude", "Laenry's Will\n===================Yes, yes, that's right, i've made a will. Don't expect too much for you Art, or Nienna for that matter. This will be a rough copy, on account of my original one being stolen in Gallmore.\nArt Akkaris\n===================Art, a dear and honest friend. You have brought adventure to my life, and for that i'm truly grateful. To you, I give my wishes that you and Clava will live a life of peace together, and bear many children. Onto more \nPhysical things, however, I leave you my warhammer. It's been a faithful weapon, and gotten me in and out of more than a few Delves, and who knows? Maybe it will serve a purpose one  day. \nOnce more, thank you Art. You, I will never forget.\nNienna Calm\n===================Nienna, the most wise and caring of all the people in the small group I can call friends. To you, I wish Relgard and yourself long and happy lives, filled with Evanta's growing laughs and smiles. To you, I leave all the \nrecipes i've invented, written in notes at the back of this book. Good luck with all future endeavours, my old friend, I know you will need it.\nGauldrim Irongut\n===================Possibly the most quirky of my friends, probably the most sociable, and definately the best bloody Dwarven friend i'll ever have. You were the one that made me first gain interest in the art of alchemy, and for that, \nI owe you a great debt. To you I wish a long life, with many adventures and many alchemical discoveries of the more fortunate nature. Also, if you ever need to use it, my hut at Leanniel is yours. Enjoy your life, Gauldrim, and thank you for helping me \nEnjoy mine.\nPolgrath\n===================The mindlord, the most powerful mage I know. You have been the figurehead of the Delvers since ever I have joined, and indeed still stand tall when many others have fallen. You have saved us all more times than I can count, \nand drawn me to yearn to learn the Arcane arts just as you have, though now I know it was fitting for me to study alchemy rather than magic.\nTo you, I hope only that all your friends stay with you in your times of need, as I fear I cannot repay you enough\nin life as I might have liked. Also, a riddle, which I hope will entertain you for at least a while.\n\nI grow stronger the longer i'm with you,\nWithout me your grow weak, To the ones that have me, Other's lives look bleak.\nSmoke\n===================The mysterious Smoke, master of shadows. We've had many adventures together, ones that I will never forget, even. You have been strong, determined and intuitive for all the years I have know you, and unlike me, the \nYears seem not to effect you at all. To you, I give my best wishes for a life filled with many more adventures, and truly hope that one day, you will trust someone enough to take off that bloody mask!\nI also leave you with a certain gift I was given by \nA man who's life I saved. It's in the chest in my hut at Leanniel. You'll know it when you see it, my friend.\nThere are so many more people that I wish I could say goodbye to, but I fear that this book is too short, and I would be long buiried by the time I wrote all I felt about those I can call my friends. \nFinal Requests\n===================\nTry not to complain too much, I don't want much.\nFirstly, though it may seem unrelated... I would like a grave made for Saxon. He was a good friend to me, and it's sad to see his passing go unnoticed today.\nSecondly, make sure everyone gets what I passed down to them in this will. All my other possessions  can be split up between the group, so feel free to bicker over them to your heart's content.\nLastly, about where to bury me; I don't really care, to be \nHonestly. By a tree would be nice, but I suppose if you want to make a statue of me I won't object.\n\nWell, that's it, my will. I hope you're all happy with what i've given you, and my passing causes as little distress as possible.\n\n*the next few pages contain all of Laenry's inventions and explorations*\n((http://tinyurl.com/boce6wk -Nightsap\n\nhttp://tinyurl.com/lgsk7nt -Burning Powder\nAging Potion\n===================1 Cup Saffvil, Crushed\n1 Cup Aqua Vitae\n1/4 Cup Swamp Blossom Nectar\n1/3 Cup Tippin's Root\n\nMethod:\n1. Bring Aqua Vitae to a simmer in a cauldron.\n2. Place Saffvil and Tippin’s Root in a woven spider string pouch, common to those used in tea making.\n3. Infuse pouch into Aqua Vitae until mixture is a sickly pink colour.\n\n4. Slowly add Swamp Blossom Nectar, drop by drop, making sure each is properly infused before adding the next one.\n5. Leave at simmering heat for one-two hours.\n6. Remove from cauldron, bottle quickly.\nNote- Adding Elrow berries, Drake’s tail, exc. Increases length of effect, not power.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lageron Vol.1", "Lageron_Greymane", "The adventures of Lageron Vol.1\n\nWritten -11th of Snow Maiden year 1444\n\nFor starters\nMy name is Lageron, just Lageron... Always been and will always be. Im a cave dwarf of Kal'Azgoth. My parents are both dead. \nMy mother died while making me enter this world. I'm not all that sure what happened to my father, but i've been told that he died in a tunnel which collapsed while he was mining in it. This was one month after my birth. I had no other relatives in the \ncity. My family had no last name due to some dishonourable act one of my ancestors had done. But as i had no relatives or place to be, the guards of Kal'azgoth took care of me. It was a good life, then i got five years old and they started training me as \na squire. Later in my life, i was asked by one of my father's mining friends, to join them and mine as my father did. I accepted the offer. I had a good life at the barracks, but i was restless to find other things than guard merchant stalls all day. \nAt that time, i was five months from being 13 years old. As i joined the miners, another of my father's old comrades gave me something. This was googles, which he said was the only thing which had been found of my father. I was fast to try them on, but as\ni put them on, the rope slit. In matter of minutes i replaced the old rope with a new one. I now put them on, they were at that time way to big for my head. But they grew to the fit during the years. \n\nThe years passed, and i had grown fond of my father's\ngoogles and friends.\nBut, shortly after i had become 26 years, me and my friends dug into a giant chasm. We found all from Diamonds to coal. And in the end of the chasm we found the thing which would change my life. It was a giant iron Cube. All rusted \nand ancient. One of my friends said not to open it. We didn't listen and opened it. Inside the cube, there was a golem. Even more rusted and ancient than the cube. Joel, one of my friends bumped into it's core with his Pickaxe. Shortly after he said it \nwas to old to activate.\nTragically he was wrong. The golem which i now refer to as rock wraiths, went into a rage. Joel was the first person struck down.\nThen another one, and it continued. I was nearly killed myself, since that day my right leg has been \ninjured. Struck with sorrow, i hit the road. Searching death and never looking back.\nI travelled far and wide, raiding caves and dungeons for something to bring me my death.\nBut, one don't know what we got before he stands with a chance to loose it. \nSo at a age of 27, i nearly got blown up by something called a creeper. I was very close to die, had it not been because of a druid. The druid was hooded and left as he had healed me. From that day, i wanted adventure, but not death.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lageron Vol.2", "Lageron_Greymane", "The adventures of Lageron Vol.2\n\nWritten -12th of Snow's Maiden Year 1444\n\nWell, this is one of my first real adventures.\nIt starts with 5 people. That would be...\nEldwehn Strongbow\nLindrianna\nOuitly\nStella\nAnd ofcourse, me...\n\nIt was the second time i ever met Eldwehn Strongbow. He had met up with a beautyfull lady named Lindrianna. She was there to meet up with a wolf Druid \nnamed Ouity and his ward which only spoke an ancient type of Elvish. Her name, is Stella. But it all started with me meeting Eldwehn. I asked him if i could join up with him for awhile. That ended up with me getting a adventure i never will forget.\nOuitly had found a cave, which he was settling into. He asked us if we wanted to join him. We said yes and from Malinor we went. Then, as we approached the cave, Eldwehn and Lindrianna searched the deepest of it. Ouitly had already been there and looted\nit. He said it contained old scrolls. But after nearly being incinerated many times, and rescued by Lindrianna and Eldwehn just as many times... We came down to a old crumbly chamber. After a while, we understood we had entered--- Guess what--- A trap...\nA spirit showed up from nowhere and ruined the way we came. I was on the other side of the lava river... for a short time... Before the bloody spirit used magic to transport me over...\nWe were sitting there. Like pigs waiting to be butchered.\nThe spirit said we could pass over if we gave a sacriflice... And that would be Lindrianna. Eldwehn and Lindrianna was best of friends, and i wasn't to kill someone. Ouitly, vanished without anyone noticing. But the spirit gave us four bottles. \nThree of them was a potion which would make us pass trough the lava unharmed. While the last would poison, and kill us.\nWe stood there for a long time before Eldwehn threw two of them out in the lava. He said he couldn't do it,\nhe was sure he had one of the poison. So, due to seeing how strongly bound Lindrianna and Eldwehn was bound by friendship... I gave him mine... Eldwehn looked down at the potion, knowing it would be the reason to my death. They both drank it, while i was \nnagging at them for using such time at it. They both passed trough unharmed and shouted back at me they would get help. I knew they couldn't get it. So i ran for it, right trough the spirit and tried to jump over the melted rocks below.\nI didn't make it. I fell right down into the lava. But trough some Cave Dwarf power i survived. I got back on the last of the bridge and the fires nearly incinerated me.\nBut as i said, i survived.\nThe rest of the story are going to be on vol.3, as well \nas how i became a Inkeeper.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lament Evenstar", "[Helmet on] Logan Garrison", " Lament for Evenstar \nEirien vi elenyr\nI laiss e-mallorn ernediaid.\n*Seens like the poem was translated*\nWhy do I linger and sing\nThere is a daisy among the elanor blossoms\nThe leaves of the mallorn are numberless\nThe brilliant star is fading", ""),
