        StaticBook("Swamp Djinn", "hex37", "  The swamp Djinn~\n\n\nD.0.C. Euclid, to safe, since it can still hurt people who stand in his way, or try to destroy it, it would still be able to fling you into a tree, or a river, or anything really...\n\n\nAppearance:\n\nA swampy monster, made out of vines, and a special looking mask, which contains all it's power, it can move around easily, and the mask looks pretty weird, Nienna might think it contains a soul, perhaps this controls it. It had a evil staff \nin his chest, which made it evil, it was a staff of dark arts who kept him evil, ripped out by Drake, the geomancer, making him a more peaceful creature, the tainted staff clearly was put there by a dark being of the north, to make this friendly giant, \ninto a killing machine, though he is more relaxed now, he wasn't back than.\nBehaviour:\n\nIt appears to protect it's mask at all cost, which can only be scraped, or even splintered by magegold, or even harder materials, it would've easily killed you if you'd approach it when it still had it's staff now, not so much.\nIt also, when it has a task, or a thing he's focused on to do, he will do it, if you get in it's way, it'll fling you away now, easily with the stretchy vines, which regenerate with ease, and very fast, it is a nice creature now, living just off the \ndelver base, living near the river at the headquarters, he will not attack, if you do not try to break the mask.\n\n\nWritten by the Geomancer Drake, of the delvers.", ""),
        StaticBook("Sweet Confession", "TheEpicFiend", "To my dearest husband, the light of my life and the apple of my eye, the greatest man I know and the perfect lover.. good morning/evening/day to you.\n\nIt's time, of course, for the next in our paper trail series as a reminder of what you mean to me.\nThough, to be honest, I'm mostly writing because I can't feel my legs. I hate that about you sometimes.\n\nIn the joking sense, naturally.\nEvery day we're apart is like the blink of an eye, time that doesn't matter. It's also like a hundred years, dragging on until I can see you again, safe and happy and as I like to put it, in one piece. It's hard to describe how much you mean to me with\njust words, so I figured I should give you something instead.\n\nI read a book the other day. Well... a week ago, at least. It's been on my mind ever since, and I guess it's really bothering me.\nIt was a book about a married couple. They  fought all the time, about the smallest of things, and eventually one ended up dying. The end of the story was.. well, horrible, as I saw it.\nIt was probably a poor choice of literature, thinking about it. It's hard to read something like that, even if I do have confidence in not having to go through that.\n\nThen it hit me.\nWe never fight, do we? I'm not sure why that bothers me, but it kind of does. Every couple fights about some things, and I can rememebr times we've fought but they all seem like distant memories now.\n\nAre there things we avoid talking about that would \nlead to fights?\n\nI think there must be. So, I got to thinking. Here it is, in writing.\n\nI want to show you all my treasures. I want to tell you all my secrets.\nI want you to know everything about me becaus I've finally managed to get it into my head that you knowing won't change anything.\n\nAnd don't worry, I'm not hiding some huge life changing secret.\nI just mean little things, like the flower my dad gave me when we first met.\n\nI love you, Ouity Deathsbane. More than anything.", ""),
        StaticBook("Sweetheart", "Timelord_Lalve", "I hope i didn't scared you with my face to face...note of man kind.\n\nWhat ever!\nI think we should meet together...discuss about future plans and such...time is short and we must make something!\nThe burning of Kastoria wasn't enough...we need to go deeper you know.\n\nYour love,\n\nThe Oogie Man.", ""),
        StaticBook("Sword Breaker", "Darkdragon274", "   >http://tinyurl.com/SwordBreaker<\n\nSwordbreaker: A medium-length dagger with large serrations, generally reaching to the line of thickness as the center of the blade. Usefull in parries.\nUsage: Catch an opponent's blade in the barbed serration, and use the leverage to disbalance or disarm the opponent. Be carefull, strong strikes or heavy weapons will be harder to counter.\nPositives\n\n-Small, concealable.\n-Good at countering enemies.\n-Serrations can cause extreme damage to flesh.\nNegatives\n\n-Not adequate for a primary weapon\n-Very specialized\n-Cannot parry heavier weapons\n-Strong or well-positioned strikes can snap the blade\nMaterial Requirements:\n\n-Two ingots of metal. \n -Stone and wood will not hold serrations\n-Wood for a hilt\n\nModifications will require additional materials.\nModifications:\n\nSpinerazor: Both sides are serrated, increasing damage against flesh, greatly reduces durability and parrying abilities.\n\n Requires an additional metal bar.\nModifications P2:\n\nAxesnapper: Smaller serrations made inside the larger ones, which are more circular. allowing a strong twist to damage wooden hafts of spears or axes. The blade is also reinforced.\n Requires and additional metal bar.", ""),
        StaticBook("Sword Plans", "Johnny_Steele", "~The Grinning Blade~\n\n\n((Written inside are the neat, but chaotically scribbled and sketched plans for a unique scimitar,))\n\n\n\nThe blade is to be made of the purest white steel, purified three times by liquidation, and once through the use of poling. It should be then worked into a rough flat sheet, a meter in length. The sheet should be heated and folded, then cooled twelve\ntimes. Any less and the  metal would bend, any more and it would be too brittle. During the folding process the metal should be worked into the form of a long wide curve. In the end it should be about half a centimeter thick, and 50 centimeters long. \nThe point should be formed by one short straight edge on foible. The other edge should be a continuous curve stretching all the way from The fuller, to the tip. From the tip to the chappe, the curve should be dramatic closer to the point, and slow as it \ngoes lower down the edge. The Crossguard, grip, and pommel should all be plain polished brass. The crossguard will be thin, about two centimeters, and long, curving down  around the grip. The grip will be ribbed, and the pommel will be a simple small\nsphere.\n\n\nWritten by \nJ- Steele", ""),
