        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "THE TRIALS\n        OF URGUAN\n\nrecounted by Bishop\nBael Tunnelsmasher\n\n1.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Dwarven Religion\nIndex One\n\nThe Brathmordakin\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nBy Bael Tunnelsmasher\n\nKey:\n\nS - Symbol\nA symbol which is related in someway to the God.\n\nA - Alignment\nThe God's system of operating in the mortal realms.\n\nP - Portfolio\nThe physical things the God is most related with.\n\nYEMEKAR\n\"The Maker\"\n\nS: Hammer + Anvil\nA: Lawful Good\nP: Creation, leadership, smithing, mining, metal, stone, fire and hard, rewarding work.\n\nYemekar is the father and creator of all. He holds a special place for smiths, miners, lumberjacks and other physical workers.\n\nHe is honored through advancement, skill, innovation and wisdom.\n\nYemekar is the most powerful being in all the cosmos.\n\nANBELLA\n\"The Hearth Mother\"\n\nS: Two Silver Rings\nA: Lawful Good\nP: Safety, honesty, marriage, children, family, water, nature, peace, home, mothers, parenthood and fertility.\n\nAnbella is the first being in all of creation, created by Yemekar before all else. As souls are forged on Yemekar's Anvil, Anbella warms and nutures them.\n\nAnbella is honored by bringing hope, health and cheer to others and by keeping oaths made.\n\nGRIMDUGAN\n\"Lord of Avarice\"\n\nS: An Obsidian Knife\nA: Chaotic Neutral\nP: Greed, money, merchants, thieves, assassins, shadows, time, avarice, cunning and patience.\n\nGrimdugan was the second creation of Yemekar. Grimdugan is the force that drives change in all things. Greed and cunning are the gifts that Grimdugan bestows on mortals.\n\nGrimdugan is honored by theft, gold and dealing with issues through alternate paths.\n\nDUNGRIMM\n\"Guardian of the Dead\"\n\nS: An Iron Mask\nA: Lawful Good\nP: Battle, valor, honor, bravery, defense, warriors, soldiers, guards, the dead and the moon.\n\nDungrimm is the younger brother of Grimdugan by a short time and commonly the most known and worshipped God of all save Yemekar himself.\n\nDungrimm is honored through battle, strife and warfare, as well as proper care of the deceased.", ""),
        StaticBook("'Electromancy'", "§bGauldrim Irongut", "ElECTROMANcY\n\nby Gauldrim Ir0nguT\n\nElectromancy is the fine art of controlling the magic of the gods themselves.\n\nIt is the element of the heavens: the God's express their wrath from the heavens through lightning.\n\nElectromancy is the harnessing of the heavens by a mortal for their own\n\nends. But before you learn, you must know the origin of the power.\n\nThe power, comes from cake.\n\nYou are now probably wondering, \"what the hell\", well you must think about it. Every power has a source. Electromancy comes from cake.\n\nIn fact, the God's\n\npull their power from a special buttercake which we will detail later on in this book.\n\nThis buttercake provides the energy and the fun and happiness to vent pure energy in the form of lightning.\n\nSo, in the beginning, you MUST bake and eat the cake!\n\nSo, if you are willing, turn the page and learn the secrets of the divine buttercake.\n\nBe ready to learn and turn the page!\n\nYes! Turn the page!\nNo! Piss off and give the book to a real chef!\n\nDivine Buttercake of the Gods!\n\nINGREDIENTS:\n2 blocks butter\n1 cup sugar\n3 eggs\n3 cups flour\n1 teaspoon yeast\nvanilla\n2/3 cups good milk\nlemon rind\n\n1. Find lightning bolt shaped cake tin. Imperative! The shape of the cake provides the power!\n\n2. Put the butter, sugar and lemon rind all together and beat until fluffy, like a storm cloud.\n\n3. Add eggs, one at a time and beating until the eggs are mixed\n\n4. Add milk and stir to combine.\n\n5. Spread the mixture into the cake tin! Evenly or your powers will be unbalanced.\n\n6. Bake for one hour and remove from heat.\n\n7. Allow the cake to stand for a six hundred count until cool.\n\n8. Dust with sugar and serve while still warm.\n\n9. Enjoy your new found cake-derived power.\n\nThere you have compeleted your electromancy cake and have the power of the God's themselves.\n\nAnd now you see why everyone enjoys \"Divine Buttercake\".\n\nIn case by know you didn't notice, we haven't actually reached the proper instructions, so skip ahead and start reading how to use the magic itself.\n\nYou must eat a cake before each casting or it will not work.\n\nYou will end up quite fat eventually.\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\n{\"text\":\"\"}\n\nIn case you didn't notice...\nThis has nothing to do with magic.\n\nWell it is to do with the magic of baking.\n\nYou fool.\n\nThank you for the Money.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lord Of The Craf", "SmileyBone224", "Day 1 by Brome UnderHill\n\nChapter 1 They Adventure Begins", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "The Veiled Shadow\n\n      My cousin and I killed him. Toveah. I now carry his necklace around my neck. I also have a key in my chest. Right now I sit in prison. I was innocent. Two men randomly started going off about \"God's work\" and mugged me. In\n\ndefense, I pulled out my axe and buried it in one of the man's arms. A small dwarf woman took me by surprise, and was led to a jail cell by some tall human while unconcious. Let's hope I can escape with my life.", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Poetry Book\n           Two\n\n           ~By Sofetios\n                 Jayamen", ""),
        StaticBook("Poetry", "§bSofetios Jayamen", "Poetry\n\n           ~By Sofetios\n                Jayamen\n\n        The Lake\n\nI wander upon a path by a lake,\nWondering where it’s all gone.\nEvery day I used to come here,\nFrom the morning to the evening's yawn.\n\nEvery now and again I wonder,\nWhy the days have passed.\nAnd think very deeply,\nWhy they don't seem to last.\n\nDay by day, night by night,\nThe Lake becomes smaller still.\nThe thoughts of it become thinner,\nEven to my strongest will.\n\nIt has become ten times smaller and ten times shallower,\nEven thought it has not changed.\nTo other people it might be normal,\nbut to me it is considered strange.\n\n       The Dreamer\n\nThe Dreamer,\nThe one who dares not be the same,\nFor in dreams mysteries unravel,\nAnd endless space you do travel,\nAs your dreams you try to tame.\n\nAs the Dreamer,\nDreams above the sky,\nAnd looks down below,\nTo the people that show,\nWho know not what lies that high.\n\nUnawakened, the Dreamer,\nIn a maze of thoughts,\nWandering the mists,\nWith endless twists,\nAs the path on minds paper he jots.\n\nThough be wary dreamer,\nAs thoughts tend to run away,\nOnly the ones you really need,\nAnd the ones you often feed,\nAre the ones that often stay.\n\n       The Cobbler\n            and\n         The Crow\n\nI was working in the night,\nAs I bent over a shoe,\nI was working very hard,\nto finish before the morning dew.\n\nI was just about finished,\nWhen what was this I hear?\nA tapping, slight tapping,\nAnd it seemed to be so near.\n\nI turned to the window,\nAnd what did I see?\nA crow tapping at my glass,\nStaring at me.\n\n\"Go away.\" I said,\nSternly as I did stand.\n\"Don't you know I have work to do?\"\nThough I know it didn't understand.\n\nBut the crow kept tapping at the glass,\nAnd I began to get mad,\nThis crow just would not stop,\nNot even a tad.\n\nI turned to my unfinished shoe,\nTo work as long as I could bear,\nBut then I noticed something missing,\nThat would stop me from completing the pair.\n\nThe golden lace that I needed,\nFor this order to be complete,\nI couldn't find it anywhere,\nAs the glass the crow did beat.\n\nI checked my table, checked my pockets,\nAnd then to my surprise,\nThere was a hole in one,\nThat was just the right size.\n\nI would never finish this shoe,\nBy the morning light,\nNow things were dire,\nand my situation was quite tight.\n\n\"I must have dropped it,\nOn the way back from the store!\"\nBut it was too far I thought,\nAs my confidence tore.\n\nBut then I realized \"What's this?\"\n\"Where was the tapping I couldn't bare?\"\nI looked to the window,\nAnd the crow wasn't there.\n\nI went to the window,\nAnd what was in its place?\nIt was simple and divine,\nThe golden lace.\n\nI opened the window,\nAnd took it with care,\nBecause of who gave this gift,\nI was certainly aware.\n\nI went to my table,\nAnd finished the shoe.\nBut then thought of the crow,\nIf I only knew!\n\nI then went to bed,\nWith not much I could say,\nI only did hope,\nThe crow would come back some day.\n\n        Friend\n           or\n          Enemy\n\nEverywhere I am\nI hide from your sight,\nIf it's in the day,\nAnd especially at night.\n\nI am one of sleep's worst enemies,\nAnd one of fear's best friends,\nI grow ever more larger,\nAs your will begins to bend.\n\nIn the day I am more friendly,\nEspecially when its hot and I'm in sight,\nBut when it turns to night time,\nI'm sure to give you a fright.\n\nI am a mimic of sorts,\nI try to copy you while you walk,\nI even mimic other people,\nAlthough I cannot talk.\n\nEven when you go about the day,\nI skulk and kind of stalk,\nFear or fear not, I am always here,\nI am the shadow on which you walk.\n\n         Imprint\n\nI stand in a forest,\nOne that I know quite well,\nWhere the trees are tall,\nAnd they never do fall,\nAt least that's what I like to tell.\n\nI came here,\nWhen I was a boy,\nUsually in the day,\nBut any time I would say,\nThe forest was my favorite toy.\n\nI came here again,\nA few years ago,\nIt was the same place I knew well,\nAs far as I could tell,\nThere was nothing different to show.\n\nI open my eyes now,\nTo see the trees,\nMy eyes see an empty place,\nAs if leaving no trace,\nThough this is not what my mind sees.\n\n   An Uncommon Sight\n\nI go about my day,\nDoing the same things as before,\nThe same old road,\nFrom my humble abode,\nNot realizing it is a bore.\n\nI walk ever onward,\nKnowing all my tasks by heart,\nA straight narrow trail,\nWith nothing new to avail,\nAll I do is my part.\n\nThen one day as I'm going past,\nA park once again,\nI see a boy,\nwithout even a toy,\nwho couldn't be past ten.\n\nThe boy was doing something abnormal,\nsomething I hadn't seen before,\nHe was looking in the sky,\nNot even blinking an eye,\nthey didn't even seem sore.\n\nI wondered if there was a hawk,\nOr something in the sky,\nI didn't hear a thing,\nOnly the birds that always sing,\nSo I did wonder why.\n\nI looked over,\nto the boy sitting by,\nHe was being bullied by his peers,\nAnd was starting to go in tears,\nJust for looking in the sky.\n\nI was about to go over,\nTo try to help if I could,\nbut the parents went first,\nBefore I feared the worst,\nTo help as they should.\n\nI walked on my way home,\nWondering why what I saw came to pass,\nI had a thought why,\nso I looked to the sky,\nand saw it in it's great mass,\n\nAs I looked to the sky,\nI was transfixed,\nI hadn't done this in a while,\nAnd it did make me smile,\nAs past and current memories mixed.\n\nI saw the vast expanse,\nIn all its mystery,\nI watched the clouds in the sky,\nAs they changed shape and passed by,\nAs they had for all of history.\n\nSuddenly someone laughed to my left,\nEven thought it was mild,\nSomeone looking at me,\nLaughing at what I appeared to be,\nJust as the others had done to the child.\n\n         Courage\n\nIt is a thing you use\nWhen you can't go to the end,\nWhen all seems lost\nAnd you can't defend.\n\nYou use it as\nIt combines with your will,\nAll you need to do is\nLet it give its fill.\n\nThe choice is yours,\nGive in or go,\nThough in the end\nOnly you will know.\n\nSee with your spirit\nAnd not with fear,\nfor then you will know\nThat courage is here.\n\nThank you for taking the time to read my poetry! I'll be writing more in the future!\n\nIf you like my writing, please let me knwo in person or by bird! \n\n~ Sofetios Jayamen\n\n\"Everything you think you know has to do with what you believe.\" ~ Sofetios", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "((A quick note; I am writing these purely for the enjoyment of others not only in Roleplay but out of the game. Please, PLEASE, do not copy these as your own. I thank you for reading what I have wrote for you.\n\nDays By Renald Briar\n\nMy Days grow old and bold\nMy beard is thick, as is the haze in my mind.\nThe sunsets along on this old day. \nLonger and Longer they grow.\nMy lips grow weaker. My mouth drys.\n--->\n\nYet your beauty remains, strong and true.\nThe righteous hue of your cream colored skin.\nYour eye as dark as the night that rushes along with the wind.\nThe sadden day I remember of the forests and the rivers. --->\n\nBut now only remains the stone of y our name and body.\nA weak piece of rock.\nStudded with the marbles of my tears.\nYour grave, it marks my end. My Days End.\n\nFin,", ""),
